1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:05,862
Chapter 48: Multifetal Pregnancy.

3
00:00:05,862 --> 00:00:31,300
Mechanisms Of Multifetal Gestations Diagnosis Of Multifetal Gestation Maternal Physiological Adaptations Pregnancy Complications Unique Fetal Complications Discordant Growth Fetal Demise Prenatal Care Preterm Birth Labor And Delivery Reducing Fetal Number References Multifetal pregnancies may result from two or more fertilization events, from a single fertilization followed by a splitting of the zygote, or both.

4
00:00:31,300 --> 00:00:39,437
In part because of infertility therapy, the twin birth rate in the United States was 3.2 percent in 2019.

5
00:00:39,437 --> 00:00:46,750
For higher-order multifetal births, the number peaked in 1998 at 0.2 percent of all births.

6
00:00:46,750 --> 00:00:53,287
Subsequent efforts in the reproductive endocrinology community to curb this rate have led to declines.

7
00:00:53,287 --> 00:01:00,912
Specifically, the rate of triplets or more declined by 55 percent from 1998 to 2019 .

8
00:01:00,912 --> 00:01:06,387
Multifetal gestations remain problematic for both the mother and her fetuses.

9
00:01:06,387 --> 00:01:15,587
For the fetus, multifetal births accounted for 3 percent of all live births in 2013 but for 15 percent of all infant deaths.

10
00:01:15,587 --> 00:01:24,375
Specifically, the infant mortality rate for twins was more than four times the rate for singletons, and for triplets it was 12-fold higher .

11
00:01:24,375 --> 00:01:32,537
Neonates from multifetal gestations also make up a disproportionate percentage of very-lowbirthweight newborns in the United States .

12
00:01:32,537 --> 00:01:39,362
These two outcomes in singletons and twins from Parkland Hospital are shown in Table 48-1.

13
00:01:39,362 --> 00:01:46,725
Risks for congenital malformations and preterm birth are greater with multifetal gestation and discussed later.

14
00:01:46,725 --> 00:01:49,225
Table 48-1.

15
00:01:49,225 --> 00:02:02,950
Selected Outcomes in Singleton and Twin Pregnancies Delivered at Parkland Hospital from 1988 through 2021 The mother may also experience higher morbidity and mortality rates, and these rise with the number of fetuses .

16
00:02:02,950 --> 00:02:15,387
In one study of more than 44,000 multifetal pregnancies, the risks for preeclampsia, postpartum hemorrhage, and maternal death were twofold higher than these rates in singleton gestations .

17
00:02:15,387 --> 00:02:20,125
Rates of placenta previa and placenta accreta spectrum are increased .

18
00:02:20,125 --> 00:02:31,412
Moreover, compared with rates for singletons, the peripartum hysterectomy rate was threefold greater for twins and 24-fold higher for triplets or quadruplets in one study .

19
00:02:31,412 --> 00:02:34,675
Mechanisms Of Multifetal Gestations.

20
00:02:34,675 --> 00:02:38,462
# Dizygotic versus Monozygotic Twinning.

21
00:02:38,462 --> 00:02:49,425
Multifetal gestations are often described by zygosity, amnionicity, and chorionicity, which are the number of zygotes, amnions, and chorions, respectively.

22
00:02:49,425 --> 00:02:57,100
Twin fetuses most often result from fertilization of two separate ova, which yields dizygotic or fraternal twins.

23
00:02:57,100 --> 00:03:04,912
Less often, twins arise from a single fertilized ovum that then divides to create monozygotic or identical twins.

24
00:03:04,912 --> 00:03:14,350
Dizygotic twins are not in a strict sense true twins because they result from the maturation and fertilization of two ova during a single ovulatory cycle.

25
00:03:14,350 --> 00:03:18,737
Genetically, these twins are like any other pair of siblings.

26
00:03:18,737 --> 00:03:22,675
Twins of opposite sex are almost always dizygotic.

27
00:03:22,675 --> 00:03:31,387
Rarely, somatic mutations or chromosome aberration in one monozygotic twin can yield differing gender karyotype or phenotype.

28
00:03:31,387 --> 00:03:41,725
For example, postzygotic loss of the Y chromosome in one 46,XY twin creates a phenotypically female twin with 45,X Turner syndrome.

29
00:03:41,725 --> 00:03:48,600
Monozygotic twins, although they have the same genetic heritage, also are usually not identical.

30
00:03:48,600 --> 00:03:57,012
For example, the division of one fertilized zygote into two does not necessarily yield equal sharing of protoplasmic material.

31
00:03:57,012 --> 00:04:09,125
In addition, monozygotic twins may be discordant for genetic mutations because of a postzygotic mutation, or they may have the same genetic disease but with marked variability in its expression.

32
00:04:09,125 --> 00:04:16,500
In female fetuses, skewed lyonization can produce differential expression of X-linked traits or diseases.

33
00:04:16,500 --> 00:04:23,475
Last, sesquizygosity is a rare event of dispermic fertilization that leads to monozygotic twins.

34
00:04:23,475 --> 00:04:26,712
Some cases may have sex discordance .

35
00:04:26,712 --> 00:04:29,475
# Monozygotic Twinning.

36
00:04:29,475 --> 00:04:34,250
Mechanisms underlying monozygotic twinning are poorly understood.

37
00:04:34,250 --> 00:04:41,337
One association is assisted reproductive technology (ART) and in-vitro fertilization (IVF).

38
00:04:41,337 --> 00:04:45,400
The monozygotic twinning incidence is twofold greater in

39
00:04:45,556 --> 00:04:51,881
pregnancies conceived using blastocyst transfer compared with transfer of a later-stage embryo.

40
00:04:51,831 --> 00:04:56,431
The predisposition to splitting may stem from culture media effects .

41
00:04:56,431 --> 00:05:03,893
The outcome of the monozygotic twinning process depends on when division occurs as depicted in Figure 48-1.

42
00:05:03,893 --> 00:05:17,656
If zygotes divide within the first 72 hours after fertilization, two embryos, two amnions, and two chorions develop, and a monozygotic, dichorionic, diamnionic twin gestation evolves.

43
00:05:17,656 --> 00:05:22,481
Two distinct placentas or a single, fused placenta may develop.

44
00:05:22,481 --> 00:05:30,718
If division occurs between days 4 through 8, a monozygotic, monochorionic, diamnionic twin pregnancy results.

45
00:05:30,718 --> 00:05:44,793
By 8 days, the chorion and the amnion have already differentiated, and division results in two embryos within a common amnionic sac, which is a monozygotic, monochorionic, monoamnionic twin gestation.

46
00:05:44,793 --> 00:05:48,556
Conjoined twins result if division initiates later.

47
00:05:48,556 --> 00:05:51,068
Figure 48-1.

48
00:05:51,068 --> 00:05:54,118
Mechanism of monozygotic twinning.

49
00:05:54,118 --> 00:06:00,143
Black boxing and blue arrows in columns A, B, and C indicate timing of division.

50
00:06:00,143 --> 00:06:06,443
A. At 0 to 4 days postfertilization, an early conceptus may divide into two.

51
00:06:06,443 --> 00:06:13,318
Division at this early stage creates two chorions and two amnions (dichorionic, diamnionic).

52
00:06:13,318 --> 00:06:16,318
Placentas may be separate or fused.

53
00:06:16,318 --> 00:06:24,368
B. Division between 4 to 8 days leads to formation of a blastocyst with two separate embryoblasts (inner cell masses).

54
00:06:24,368 --> 00:06:31,243
Each embryoblast will form its own amnion within a shared chorion (monochorionic, diamnionic).

55
00:06:31,243 --> 00:06:38,043
C. Between 8 and 12 days, the amnion and amnionic cavity form above the germinal disc.

56
00:06:38,043 --> 00:06:45,956
Embryonic division leads to two embryos with a shared amnion and shared chorion (monochorionic, monoamnionic).

57
00:06:45,956 --> 00:06:50,143
D. Differing theories explain conjoined twin development.

58
00:06:50,143 --> 00:06:54,493
One describes an incomplete splitting of one embryo into two.

59
00:06:54,493 --> 00:07:00,531
The other describes fusion of a portion of one embryo from a monozygotic pair onto the other.

60
00:07:00,531 --> 00:07:04,568
Rarely, monochorionic twins may be dizygotic.

61
00:07:04,568 --> 00:07:06,993
Mechanisms are unclear.

62
00:07:06,993 --> 00:07:16,143
In one review of 31 cases, nearly 80 percent were conceived after ART procedures, and 90 percent were associated with chimerism .

63
00:07:16,143 --> 00:07:19,031
# Factors Affecting Twinning.

64
00:07:19,031 --> 00:07:32,118
Dizygotic twinning is much more common than monozygotic splitting of a single oocyte, and its incidence is positively influenced by infertility treatment and by maternal age, race, heredity, and size.

65
00:07:32,118 --> 00:07:41,656
By contrast, the frequency of monozygotic twin births is relatively constant worldwide— approximately one set per 250 births.

66
00:07:41,656 --> 00:07:47,456
This incidence is generally independent of most demographic factors, except ART.

67
00:07:47,456 --> 00:08:03,131
Of infertility therapies, ovulation induction with clomiphene citrate or with follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) plus human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) remarkably enhances the likelihood of multiple concurrent ovulations.

68
00:08:03,131 --> 00:08:10,406
Moreover, with IVF, the greater the number of embryos that are transferred, the greater the risk of twins or more.

69
00:08:10,406 --> 00:08:21,693
In 2017, ART contributed to 1.9 percent of all newborns in the United States and to 14.7 percent of all neonates in multifetal gestations .

70
00:08:21,693 --> 00:08:35,243
The American Society for Reproductive Medicine (2017) revised their guidelines regarding the number of embryos or blastocysts transferred during IVF to reduce the incidence of multifetal pregnancies.

71
00:08:35,243 --> 00:08:43,468
For example, women younger than 35 years are encouraged to receive a single-embryo transfer, regardless of embryo stage.

72
00:08:43,468 --> 00:08:47,668
These practices have effectively lowered multifetal rates .

73
00:08:47,668 --> 00:08:56,643
Advancing maternal age and delayed childbearing are other important risk factors (Adashi, 2018; Otta, 2016).

74
00:08:56,643 --> 00:09:03,531
One explanation is FSH levels, which rise with age and lead to greater ovarian stimulation .

75
00:09:03,531 --> 00:09:07,893
Higher levels of FSH have been linked with dizygotic twinning .

76
00:09:07,893 --> 00:09:12,318
Another explanation may be a higher use of ART in older women.

77
00:09:12,318 --> 00:09:17,581
Paternal age has also been linked to twinning, but its effect is felt to be small .

78
00:09:17,581 --> 00:09:22,831
Different races and ethnic groups vary in their frequency of multifetal births.

79
00:09:22,831 --> 00:09:33,906
In the United States in 2019, the twin birth rate was 4.1 percent in black women, 3.3 percent in whites, and 2.5 in Hispanics .

80
00:09:33,906 --> 00:09:39,568
In one rural community in Nigeria, twinning occurred once in every 20 births .

81
00:09:39,568 --> 00:09:42,956
These marked differences in twinning frequency

82
00:09:43,108 --> 00:09:47,245
may stem from racial variations in FSH levels .

83
00:09:47,195 --> 00:09:53,108
Heredity is another factor, and maternal influence appears to supersede that of the father.

84
00:09:53,108 --> 00:10:02,733
In a study of 4000 genealogical records, women who themselves were a dizygotic twin gave birth to twins at a rate of one set per 58 births .

85
00:10:02,733 --> 00:10:11,908
Women who were not a twin, but whose husbands were a dizygotic twin, gave birth to twins at a rate of one set per 116 pregnancies.

86
00:10:11,908 --> 00:10:19,770
Genome-wide association studies have identified potentially contributory genes, and two are related to FSH .

87
00:10:19,770 --> 00:10:25,945
However, the contribution of these variants to the overall incidence of twinning is likely small .

88
00:10:25,945 --> 00:10:29,020
Maternal size is another risk factor.

89
00:10:29,020 --> 00:10:36,220
Nylander (1971) showed an increasing gradient in the twinning rate in taller, heavier women.

90
00:10:36,220 --> 00:10:42,658
These had a twinning rate 25 to 30 percent higher than short, nutritionally deprived women.

91
00:10:42,658 --> 00:10:49,070
Likewise, another study found an association between greater maternal weight and dizygotic twinning .

92
00:10:49,070 --> 00:10:56,683
Evidence acquired during and after World War II suggests that twinning correlates more with nutrition than with body size.

93
00:10:56,683 --> 00:11:03,883
Widespread undernourishment in Europe during those years was associated with a marked fall in the dizygotic twinning rate .

94
00:11:03,883 --> 00:11:07,558
# Superfecundation and Superfetation.

95
00:11:07,558 --> 00:11:16,620
Superfecundation is fertilization of two ova within the same menstrual cycle but not at the same coitus nor necessarily by sperm from the same male.

96
00:11:16,620 --> 00:11:19,520
The latter leads to heteropaternity .

97
00:11:19,520 --> 00:11:26,845
In superfetation, an interval as long as or longer than a menstrual cycle intervenes between fertilizations.

98
00:11:26,845 --> 00:11:32,208
Superfetation is not known to occur spontaneously and is likely due to ART .

99
00:11:32,208 --> 00:11:39,070
Pseudosuperfetation often results from markedly unequal growth of twins with the same gestational age.

100
00:11:39,070 --> 00:11:42,245
Diagnosis Of Multifetal Gestation.

101
00:11:42,245 --> 00:11:45,083
# Clinical Evaluation.

102
00:11:45,083 --> 00:11:50,670
Early diagnosis can help with management of the associated risks posed by twins.

103
00:11:50,670 --> 00:11:54,470
Accurate fundal height measurement can be an initial tool.

104
00:11:54,470 --> 00:12:01,495
With multiples, uterine size is typically larger during the second trimester than that expected for a singleton.

105
00:12:01,495 --> 00:12:10,408
In one study, fundal heights obtained between 20 and 30 weeks’ gestation averaged 5 cm greater in twins than in singletons .

106
00:12:10,408 --> 00:12:16,058
Palpation of fetal parts to diagnose twins before the third trimester is difficult.

107
00:12:16,058 --> 00:12:20,370
Even then, obesity or hydramnios can hinder assessment.

108
00:12:20,370 --> 00:12:24,783
Palpating two fetal heads strongly supports the diagnosis.

109
00:12:24,783 --> 00:12:33,808
Moreover, a hand-held Doppler ultrasonic unit may isolate two fetal heartbeats if their rates are clearly distinct from each other and from the mother.

110
00:12:33,808 --> 00:12:40,983
Overall, however, clinical criteria alone to diagnose multifetal gestations is unreliable.

111
00:12:40,983 --> 00:12:55,708
In the Routine Antenatal Diagnostic Imaging with Ultrasound (RADIUS) trial, for 37 percent of women who did not have a screening ultrasound examination, their twin pregnancy was not diagnosed until 26 weeks’ gestation.

112
00:12:55,708 --> 00:13:03,433
In 13 percent of unscanned women, their multifetal gestation was identified only during their admission for delivery .

113
00:13:03,433 --> 00:13:05,745
# Sonography.

114
00:13:05,745 --> 00:13:10,683
Sonographic examination should detect practically all sets of twins.

115
00:13:10,683 --> 00:13:18,220
Further, it should aim to determine fetal number, estimated gestational age, chorionicity, and amnionicity.

116
00:13:18,220 --> 00:13:27,533
Importantly, the risk for many twin-specific complications varies in relation to these (Hack, 2008; Manning, 1995).

117
00:13:27,533 --> 00:13:29,995
Table 48-2.

118
00:13:29,995 --> 00:13:40,695
Incidence of Some Complications Related to Twin Characteristics With sonography, separate gestational sacs, if present, can be identified early in twin pregnancy .

119
00:13:40,695 --> 00:13:49,108
Subsequently, each fetal head should be seen in two perpendicular planes to avoid mistaking a fetal trunk for a second fetal head.

120
00:13:49,108 --> 00:13:58,333
Ideally, two fetal heads or two abdomens are seen in the same image plane to avoid scanning the same fetus twice and interpreting it as twins.

121
00:13:58,333 --> 00:14:03,208
Higher-order multifetal gestations are more challenging to evaluate.

122
00:14:03,208 --> 00:14:09,995
Even in the first trimester, identifying the actual number of fetuses and their position can be difficult.

123
00:14:09,995 --> 00:14:16,670
This determination is especially important if selective reduction is considered (p. 858).

124
00:14:16,670 --> 00:14:19,170
Figure 48-2.

125
00:14:19,170 --> 00:14:22,320
Sonograms of first-trimester twins.

126
00:14:22,320 --> 00:14:27,533
A. Dichorionic diamnionic twin pregnancy at 6 weeks’ gestation.

127
00:14:27,533 --> 00:14:29,708
Note the thick dividing

128
00:14:29,860 --> 00:14:32,297
chorion (yellow arrow).

129
00:14:32,247 --> 00:14:35,960
One of the yolk sacs is indicated (blue arrow).

130
00:14:35,960 --> 00:14:41,235
B. Monochorionic diamnionic twin pregnancy at 8 weeks’ gestation.

131
00:14:41,235 --> 00:14:47,997
Note the thin amnion encircling each embryo, resulting in a thin dividing membrane (blue arrow).

132
00:14:47,997 --> 00:14:54,735
In determining chorionicity, sonography’s accuracy diminishes as gestational age advances.

133
00:14:54,735 --> 00:15:03,060
It has a 98-percent accuracy in the first trimester but may be incorrect in up to 10 percent of second-trimester examinations.

134
00:15:03,060 --> 00:15:15,572
Overall, chorionicity can be correctly determined with sonography before 24 weeks in approximately 95 percent of cases (Emery, 2015; Lee, 2006).

135
00:15:15,572 --> 00:15:21,835
The sonographic features that are used to determine chorionicity vary according to gestational age.

136
00:15:21,835 --> 00:15:28,022
Early in the first trimester, the number of chorions equates to the number of gestational sacs.

137
00:15:28,022 --> 00:15:38,060
A thick band of chorion that separates two gestational sacs signals a dichorionic pregnancy, whereas monochorionic twins have a single gestational sac.

138
00:15:38,060 --> 00:15:46,997
If the gestation is monochorionic diamnionic, it may be difficult to visualize the thin intervening amnion before 8 weeks’ gestation.

139
00:15:46,997 --> 00:15:54,760
If the intervening membrane is difficult to visualize, the number of yolk sacs usually correlates with the number of amnions.

140
00:15:54,760 --> 00:16:01,047
However, the number of yolk sacs as a predictor of amnionicity may not always be accurate .

141
00:16:01,047 --> 00:16:07,397
Although uncommonly seen early, cord entanglement identifies a monoamnionic gestation.

142
00:16:07,397 --> 00:16:15,035
At 10 to 14 weeks’ gestation, sonographic assessment of chorionicity may be determined using four features.

143
00:16:15,035 --> 00:16:27,897
These are the number of placental masses, presence of an intervening membrane dividing the sacs, thickness of that membrane, and fetal gender (Emery, 2015; Khalil, 2016).

144
00:16:27,897 --> 00:16:32,197
First, two separate placentas suggest dichorionicity.

145
00:16:32,197 --> 00:16:38,247
The converse is not necessarily true, such as cases with a single fused placental mass.

146
00:16:38,247 --> 00:16:48,347
Second, identification of a thick dividing membrane—generally ≥2 mm—supports a presumed diagnosis of dichorionicity.

147
00:16:48,347 --> 00:16:56,410
In a dichorionic pregnancy, this visualized membrane is composed of four layers—two amnions and two chorions.

148
00:16:56,410 --> 00:17:06,422
Also, the twin peak sign—also called lambda or delta sign—is seen by examining the point of origin of the dividing membrane on the placental surface.

149
00:17:06,422 --> 00:17:14,147
The peak appears as a triangular projection of placental tissue extending a short distance between the layers of the dividing membrane .

150
00:17:14,147 --> 00:17:16,685
Figure 48-3.

151
00:17:16,685 --> 00:17:23,760
A. Sonographic image of the “twin-peak” sign, also termed the “lambda sign,” in a 24week gestation.

152
00:17:23,760 --> 00:17:31,297
At the top of this sonogram, tissue from the anterior placenta is seen extending downward between the amnion layers.

153
00:17:31,297 --> 00:17:34,560
This sign confirms dichorionic twinning.

154
00:17:34,560 --> 00:17:39,310
B. The “twin-peak” sign is seen at the bottom of this schematic diagram.

155
00:17:39,310 --> 00:17:44,910
The triangular portion of placenta insinuates between the amniochorion layers.

156
00:17:44,910 --> 00:17:55,485
In contrast, monochorionic pregnancies have a dividing membrane that is so thin (generally <2 mm) that it may not be seen until the second trimester.

157
00:17:55,485 --> 00:18:03,735
The relationship between the membranes and placenta without apparent extension of placenta between the dividing membranes is called the T sign .

158
00:18:03,735 --> 00:18:15,985
Evaluation of the dividing membrane can establish chorionicity in more than 99 percent of pregnancies in the first trimester (Maruotti, 2016; Miller, 2012).

159
00:18:15,985 --> 00:18:21,410
Lack of a dividing membrane signals a monochorionic monoamnionic gestation.

160
00:18:21,410 --> 00:18:24,072
Figure 48-4.

161
00:18:24,072 --> 00:18:30,397
A. Sonographic image of the “T” sign in a monochorionic diamnionic gestation at 30 weeks.

162
00:18:30,397 --> 00:18:37,060
At the top of this sonogram, the two apposed amnion layers are seen as the thin line extending downward.

163
00:18:37,060 --> 00:18:40,285
B. Schematic diagram of the “T” sign.

164
00:18:40,285 --> 00:18:46,285
Twins are separated only by a membrane created by the juxtaposed amnion of each twin.

165
00:18:46,285 --> 00:18:50,510
A “T” is formed at the point at which amnions meet the placenta.

166
00:18:50,510 --> 00:18:56,785
Last, twins with differing gender indicates a dichorionic (and dizygotic) gestation.

167
00:18:56,785 --> 00:19:01,322
Rare exceptions were described earlier (p. 839).

168
00:19:01,322 --> 00:19:04,335
# Other Diagnostic Aids.

169
00:19:04,335 --> 00:19:12,747
Magnetic resonance (MR) imaging may help delineate complications in monochorionic twins, including conjoined twins .

170
00:19:12,747 --> 00:19:19,435
Abdominal radiography can be used if fetal number in a higher-order multifetal gestation is uncertain.

171
00:19:19,588 --> 00:19:27,913
However, radiographs generally have limited utility and lead to an incorrect diagnosis if fetuses move during the exposure.

172
00:19:27,863 --> 00:19:33,125
No biochemical test reliably identifies multifetal gestations.

173
00:19:33,125 --> 00:19:43,013
Serum levels of β-hCG and of maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein (MSAFP) are usually higher, but ranges may overlap with those of singletons.

174
00:19:43,013 --> 00:19:45,925
# Placental Examination.

175
00:19:45,925 --> 00:19:54,313
Careful visual examination of the placenta and membranes after delivery can establish zygosity and chorionicity in many cases.

176
00:19:54,313 --> 00:20:00,450
First, the placenta is gently delivered to preserve the attachment of the amnion and chorion.

177
00:20:00,450 --> 00:20:10,438
With one common amnionic sac or with juxtaposed amnions not separated by chorion, the fetuses are monozygotic (see Fig. 48-1).

178
00:20:10,438 --> 00:20:19,263
If adjacent amnions are separated by chorion, the fetuses could be either dizygotic or monozygotic, but dizygosity is more common .

179
00:20:19,263 --> 00:20:25,125
If the neonates are of the same sex, blood typing of cord blood samples may be helpful.

180
00:20:25,125 --> 00:20:34,000
Different genders or blood types reflects dizygosity, although the same gender or blood type in each fetus does not confirm monozygosity.

181
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:44,025
Postnatal zygosity genetic testing is available, and the benefits and ethics have been debated (Brown, 2015; Craig, 2015).

182
00:20:44,025 --> 00:20:46,700
Figure 48-5.

183
00:20:46,700 --> 00:20:50,163
Dichorionic diamnionic twin placenta.

184
00:20:50,163 --> 00:20:58,413
The membrane partition that separated twin fetuses is elevated and consists of chorion (c) between two amnions (a).

185
00:20:58,413 --> 00:21:01,675
Maternal Physiological Adaptations.

186
00:21:01,675 --> 00:21:11,500
The physiological burdens of pregnancy and likelihood of serious maternal complications are typically greater with multifetal gestations than with singleton ones.

187
00:21:11,500 --> 00:21:19,800
In the first trimester and with its higher serum β-hCG levels, multifetal gestations often cause nausea and vomiting.

188
00:21:19,800 --> 00:21:30,200
In women carrying more than one fetus, blood volume expansion is greater and averages 50 to 60 percent compared with 40 to 50 percent in those with a singleton .

189
00:21:30,200 --> 00:21:38,875
This augmented hypervolemia teleologically offsets blood loss with vaginal delivery of twins, which is twice that with a single fetus.

190
00:21:38,875 --> 00:21:44,988
Although red cell mass also accrues, it does so proportionately less in twin pregnancies.

191
00:21:44,988 --> 00:21:50,513
Combined with greater iron and folate requirements, this predisposes to anemia.

192
00:21:50,513 --> 00:21:55,550
Women carrying twins also have a typical pattern of blood pressure change.

193
00:21:55,550 --> 00:22:02,050
One study assessed serial blood pressures in more than 13,000 singleton and twin pregnancies .

194
00:22:02,050 --> 00:22:10,038
As early as 8 weeks’ gestation, the diastolic blood pressure in women with twins was lower than that with singleton pregnancies.

195
00:22:10,038 --> 00:22:13,450
It generally rose by a greater degree at term.

196
00:22:13,450 --> 00:22:24,963
An earlier study demonstrated that this later rise was at least 15 mm Hg in 95 percent of women with twins compared with only 54 percent of women with a singleton .

197
00:22:24,963 --> 00:22:31,488
Hypervolemia along with decreased vascular resistance has an impressive effect on cardiac function.

198
00:22:31,488 --> 00:22:40,025
In one study of 119 women with twins, cardiac output rose another 20 percent above that in women with a singleton .

199
00:22:40,025 --> 00:22:51,050
Similarly, a study of serial maternal echocardiography examinations found a greater elevation in cardiac output in 20 women with uncomplicated twin pregnancies .

200
00:22:51,050 --> 00:22:58,850
Both studies found that the augmented cardiac output was predominantly due to greater stroke volume rather than higher heart rate.

201
00:22:58,850 --> 00:23:06,050
Vascular resistance was significantly lower in twin gestations throughout pregnancy compared with singleton ones.

202
00:23:06,050 --> 00:23:16,300
In a study of 30 uncomplicated twin pregnancies, this same group of investigators identified progressive diastolic dysfunction from the first to third trimester.

203
00:23:16,300 --> 00:23:20,250
The dysfunction subsequently normalized after delivery .

204
00:23:20,250 --> 00:23:26,200
Uterine growth in a multifetal gestation is substantively greater than in a singleton one.

205
00:23:26,200 --> 00:23:33,488
The uterus and its nonfetal contents may achieve a volume of 10 L or more and weigh in excess of 20 pounds.

206
00:23:33,488 --> 00:23:40,413
Especially with monozygotic twins, excessive amounts of amnionic fluid may rapidly accumulate.

207
00:23:40,413 --> 00:23:48,688
In these circumstances, maternal abdominal viscera and lungs can be appreciably compressed and displaced by the expanding uterus.

208
00:23:48,688 --> 00:23:55,738
As a result, the size and weight of the large uterus may preclude more than a sedentary existence for these women.

209
00:23:55,738 --> 00:24:00,875
Rarely, maternal renal function can become seriously impaired, most

210
00:24:01,036 --> 00:24:04,748
likely as the consequence of obstructive uropathy .

211
00:24:04,698 --> 00:24:18,048
Hydramnios is a common associate, and therapeutic amniocentesis may provide relief for the mother, may improve obstructive uropathy, and possibly may lower rates of preterm labor or rupture of membranes.

212
00:24:18,048 --> 00:24:24,373
Unfortunately, hydramnios often develops remote from term and rapidly reaccumulates.

213
00:24:24,373 --> 00:24:26,811
Pregnancy Complications.

214
00:24:26,811 --> 00:24:32,348
Several complications, discussed next, complicate multifetal pregnancy.

215
00:24:32,348 --> 00:24:38,461
Others are preterm birth and discordant fetal growth, which are discussed in later sections.

216
00:24:38,461 --> 00:24:42,498
# Spontaneous Abortion and Vanishing Fetus.

217
00:24:42,498 --> 00:24:52,186
Among twins, monochorionic twins have significantly higher early fetal loss rates than dichorionic pairs (D’Antonio, 2013).

218
00:24:52,186 --> 00:24:58,611
Twins achieved through ART may be at greater risk for abortion compared with those conceived spontaneously .

219
00:24:58,611 --> 00:25:04,973
However, among ART-conceived gestations, twins have lower miscarriage rates than singletons .

220
00:25:04,973 --> 00:25:09,561
In some cases, only one fetus is spontaneously lost.

221
00:25:09,561 --> 00:25:16,486
As a result, the incidence of twins in the first trimester is much greater than the incidence of twins at birth.

222
00:25:16,486 --> 00:25:23,686
Estimates suggest that while 1 in 8 pregnancies begin multifetal, only 1 in 80 births are multifetal .

223
00:25:23,686 --> 00:25:37,261
Sonography studies in the first trimester have shown that one twin dies and “vanishes” before the second trimester in 10 to 40 percent of all twin pregnancies (Brady, 2013; Harris, 2020).

224
00:25:37,261 --> 00:25:40,723
The incidence is higher following ART conception.

225
00:25:40,723 --> 00:25:44,873
A vanishing fetus is more common in higher-order multiples.

226
00:25:44,873 --> 00:26:02,461
In one study of spontaneous reduction in 709 multifetal pregnancies before 12 weeks’ gestation, one or more embryos died in 36 percent of twin pregnancies, in 53 percent of triplet pregnancies, and in 65 percent of quadruplet pregnancies .

227
00:26:02,461 --> 00:26:13,873
Interestingly, in one study, ultimate pregnancy duration and birthweight were inversely related to initial gestational sac number regardless of the final number of fetuses at delivery .

228
00:26:13,873 --> 00:26:18,511
This effect was most pronounced in twins who started as quadruplets.

229
00:26:18,511 --> 00:26:30,123
Evidence for adverse immediate and long-term effects of twin spontaneous reduction on the remaining pregnancy is conflicting (Harris, 2020; McNamara, 2016).

230
00:26:30,123 --> 00:26:35,711
Spontaneous reduction of a twin gestation may affect prenatal screening results.

231
00:26:35,711 --> 00:26:45,936
In one study of ART-conceived gestations, 56 twin pregnancies with a single early demise and 897 singleton gestations were compared .

232
00:26:45,936 --> 00:26:54,811
First-trimester serum marker concentrations did not differ between the groups if the embryonic loss was identified before 9 weeks’ gestation.

233
00:26:54,811 --> 00:27:00,211
If diagnosed after 9 weeks, the marker levels were higher and less precise.

234
00:27:00,211 --> 00:27:09,348
With a vanishing twin, first-trimester maternal serum levels of the pregnancyassociated plasma protein A (PAPP-A) can be elevated.

235
00:27:09,348 --> 00:27:14,398
Second-trimester MSAFP and dimeric inhibin A levels also can be higher .

236
00:27:14,398 --> 00:27:22,123
Early loss of one twin may also affect noninvasive prenatal testing using cell-free DNA (cfDNA) .

237
00:27:22,123 --> 00:27:28,136
In one report, 15 percent of the false-positive results were attributed to this effect .

238
00:27:28,136 --> 00:27:37,048
The development of single-nucleotide polymorphism technology for cfDNA testing holds promise in better identifying these cases .

239
00:27:37,048 --> 00:27:46,486
Thus, a spontaneously reduced abortus is ideally identified to help avoid confusion with results from aneuploidy and neural-tube defect screening.

240
00:27:46,486 --> 00:27:49,523
# Congenital Malformations.

241
00:27:49,523 --> 00:27:55,786
The incidence of these is appreciably higher in multifetal gestations compared with singleton ones.

242
00:27:55,786 --> 00:28:05,348
In one study, the congenital malformation rate was 406 per 10,000 twins compared with 238 per 10,000 singletons .

243
00:28:05,348 --> 00:28:11,448
This rate in monochorionic twins was almost twice that of dichorionic twin gestations.

244
00:28:11,448 --> 00:28:21,098
One large study between 1998 and 2010 found that twins had a 73-percent greater risk of congenital heart disease than singletons.

245
00:28:21,098 --> 00:28:30,836
The occurrence risk and concordance were substantially higher among monochorionic twins (Best, 2015; Gijtenbeek, 2019).

246
00:28:30,836 --> 00:28:42,911
From a 30-year European registry of multifetal births, structural anomaly rates rose steadily from 2.2 percent in 1987 to 3.3 percent in 2007 .

247
00:28:42,911 --> 00:28:52,023
Yet, during this time, the proportion of dizygotic twins grew by 30 percent, whereas the proportion of monozygotic twins remained stable.

248
00:28:52,180 --> 00:28:59,880
This higher risk of congenital malformations in dizygotic twins over time correlated with increased availability of ART.

249
00:28:59,830 --> 00:29:04,480
An increase in birth defect rates related to ART has been reported .

250
00:29:04,480 --> 00:29:14,030
However, if data are adjusted for maternal age or duration of subfertility, the risk of congenital anomalies does not appear to be increased by ART .

251
00:29:14,030 --> 00:29:16,417
# Low Birthweight.

252
00:29:16,417 --> 00:29:24,905
Multifetal gestations are more likely to be low birthweight than singleton pregnancies due to restricted fetal growth and preterm delivery.

253
00:29:24,905 --> 00:29:31,355
Birthweights in twins closely paralleled those of singletons until 28 to 30 weeks’ gestation.

254
00:29:31,355 --> 00:29:35,267
Thereafter, twin birthweights progressively lagged .

255
00:29:35,267 --> 00:29:42,617
Beginning at 35 to 36 weeks’ gestation, twin birthweights clearly diverge from those of singletons.

256
00:29:42,617 --> 00:29:49,905
Thus, abnormal growth should be diagnosed only when fetal size is less than expected for multifetal gestation.

257
00:29:49,905 --> 00:29:59,617
Accordingly, twin and triplet growth curves have been developed (Kim, 2010; Odibo, 2013; Vora, 2006).

258
00:29:59,617 --> 00:30:08,217
To confirm suitable growth in dichorionic pairs, we perform sonography every 4 weeks, starting at 16 to 20 weeks.

259
00:30:08,217 --> 00:30:15,455
Monochorionic twins are imaged every 2 weeks for twin-twin transfusion syndrome (p. 848).

260
00:30:15,455 --> 00:30:24,430
To identify suspected fetal-growth restriction, we use the standards of birthweight for twin gestations stratified by placental chorionicity .

261
00:30:24,430 --> 00:30:27,130
Figure 48-6.

262
00:30:27,130 --> 00:30:43,955
Birthweight percentiles (25th to 75th) for 357,205 singleton neonates compared with the 50th birthweight percentile for 3714 twins, Parkland Hospital 1988–2012.

263
00:30:43,955 --> 00:30:54,142
Infants with major malformations, pregnancies complicated by stillbirth, and twin gestations with >25 percent discordance also were excluded.

264
00:30:54,142 --> 00:30:56,980
Data from Dr. Don McIntire.

265
00:30:56,980 --> 00:30:59,330
# Hypertension.

266
00:30:59,330 --> 00:31:11,580
Compared with mothers of singletons, those with multifetal gestations are more likely to develop a pregnancy-associated hypertensive disorder, and the incidence further rises with advancing fetal number .

267
00:31:11,580 --> 00:31:18,655
In one analysis, 14 percent of parturients with twins developed a hypertensive disorder of pregnancy .

268
00:31:18,655 --> 00:31:25,467
No specific zygosity confers a greater rate of these disorders (Lučovnik, 2016).

269
00:31:25,467 --> 00:31:31,767
However, with twins and gestational diabetes, the preeclampsia incidence is increased .

270
00:31:31,767 --> 00:31:38,817
Similar to singleton pregnancies with hypertensive disorders, fetal-growth restriction is a potential outcome .

271
00:31:38,817 --> 00:31:47,580
Data suggest that rising fetal number and levels of antiangiogenic factor play roles in preeclampsia pathogenesis in multiples .

272
00:31:47,580 --> 00:32:05,067
Nonhypertensive women with twins compared with those with singletons have higher levels of the antiangiogenic factors soluble fms-like tyrosine kinase 1 (sFlt-1) and soluble endoglin and lower levels of proangiogenic placental growth factor (PlGF) .

273
00:32:05,067 --> 00:32:19,217
In women with twins and preeclampsia in one study, both sFlt-1 levels and sFlt-1/PlGF ratios rose and PlGF concentrations declined compared with normotensive women with twins .

274
00:32:19,217 --> 00:32:27,067
Aspirin therapy is recommended to help prevent preeclampsia and is discussed with prenatal care (p. 853).

275
00:32:27,067 --> 00:32:30,180
# Long-term Infant Development.

276
00:32:30,180 --> 00:32:42,917
In cohort studies evaluating twin and singletons, academic performance is similar and is unaffected by ART-assisted conception (Christensen, 2006; Spangmose, 2017).

277
00:32:42,917 --> 00:32:49,992
For mono- or dichorionic pairs born preterm, neurodevelopment at 2 years was reported to be similar .

278
00:32:49,992 --> 00:32:59,942
However, among normal-birthweight neonates, the cerebral palsy (CP) risk is twofold higher among multifetal pregnancies compared with singletons.

279
00:32:59,942 --> 00:33:13,255
In term and preterm neonates from 12 large CP registries, the overall CP rate was 1.6 per 1000 singleton live births and 7 per 1000 live births from multifetal pregnancies .

280
00:33:13,255 --> 00:33:21,267
In this and another registry study, the higher CP rate in multifetal gestations was attributed mainly to prematurity .

281
00:33:21,267 --> 00:33:30,405
Fetal-growth restriction, congenital anomalies, twin-twin transfusion syndrome, and fetal demise of a co-twin are other potential contributors.

282
00:33:30,405 --> 00:33:33,030
Unique Fetal Complications.

283
00:33:33,030 --> 00:33:37,630
Several unique complications arise in multifetal pregnancies.

284
00:33:37,630 --> 00:33:43,567
These are described in twins but also can be found in higher-order multifetal gestations.

285
00:33:43,567 --> 00:33:50,205
Most fetal complications attributed to the twinning process itself are seen with monozygotic twins.

286
00:33:50,356 --> 00:33:57,118
Their pathogenesis is best understood after reviewing the possibilities shown in Figure 48-1.

287
00:33:57,068 --> 00:34:00,056
# Monoamnionic Twins.

288
00:34:00,056 --> 00:34:10,443
These twins make up less than 1 percent of all twin pregnancies and 4 percent of monochorionic pairs (Chitrit, 2021; Sebire, 2000).

289
00:34:10,443 --> 00:34:15,118
However, they have high rates of many significant complications.

290
00:34:15,118 --> 00:34:19,356
Fetal loss rates in monoamnionic twins are substantial.

291
00:34:19,356 --> 00:34:26,181
Among fetuses alive before 16 weeks’ gestation, less than half survive to the neonatal period.

292
00:34:26,181 --> 00:34:31,593
Fetal abnormalities and spontaneous miscarriage contribute to most losses .

293
00:34:31,593 --> 00:34:45,431
A high perinatal death rate is attributable to preterm birth, twin-twin transfusion syndrome, cord entanglement, birthweight discordance, and congenital anomalies (Buca, 2020; Saccone, 2020).

294
00:34:45,431 --> 00:34:51,106
Congenital anomaly rates in monoamnionic twins reach 18 to 28 percent .

295
00:34:51,106 --> 00:34:56,393
Concordance of anomalies is found in only approximately one quarter of cases.

296
00:34:56,393 --> 00:35:02,556
Because of the greater risk of cardiac anomalies, fetal echocardiography is indicated.

297
00:35:02,556 --> 00:35:11,243
Of note, monoamnionic twins are by definition monozygotic and thus presumed to be genetically identical, except in rare cases .

298
00:35:11,243 --> 00:35:20,831
Interestingly, the risk for Down syndrome in each fetus of the monozygotic pair is similar to or lower than the risk in maternal age-matched singletons .

299
00:35:20,831 --> 00:35:28,518
Twin-twin transfusion syndrome rates in monoamnionic twins are lower than in monochorionic diamnionic pregnancies .

300
00:35:28,518 --> 00:35:37,543
This may be due to the near universal presence in monoamnionic twins of arterioarterial anastomoses, which are presumed to be protective .

301
00:35:37,543 --> 00:35:44,243
Nonetheless, twin-twin transfusion syndrome surveillance is recommended and described in that section.

302
00:35:44,243 --> 00:35:47,843
Umbilical cord entanglement is a frequent event .

303
00:35:47,843 --> 00:35:56,843
Although color-flow Doppler sonography can help diagnose entanglement, factors that lead to pathological umbilical vessel constriction are unknown.

304
00:35:56,843 --> 00:36:03,818
Fetal death from cord entanglement is thus unpredictable, and monitoring for this is relatively ineffective.

305
00:36:03,818 --> 00:36:11,956
Moreover, studies of inpatient or outpatient fetal surveillance of monoamnionic twin pregnancies show conflicting data.

306
00:36:11,956 --> 00:36:22,718
Heyborne and coworkers (2005) reported no stillbirths in 43 women admitted at 26 to 27 weeks’ gestation for daily fetal surveillance.

307
00:36:22,718 --> 00:36:28,518
Conversely, 13 fetuses died in 44 women managed as outpatients.

308
00:36:28,518 --> 00:36:47,056
In the MONOMONO Working Group (2019) study of 195 women with these twins, the fetal death rate was 3.3 percent in 75 mothers admitted at 24 to 29 weeks’ gestation compared with 10.8 percent in the 120 gravidas monitored outpatient.

309
00:36:47,056 --> 00:36:52,668
Notably, no fetal deaths occurred after 32 weeks’ gestation in either group.

310
00:36:52,668 --> 00:37:08,156
Last, Van Mieghem and associates (2014) found the risk of “potentially preventable death” was not significantly different in women with inpatient (2.1 percent) or outpatient (4.7 percent) fetal surveillance.

311
00:37:08,156 --> 00:37:10,831
Figure 48-7.

312
00:37:10,831 --> 00:37:14,743
Monochorionic monoamnionic cord entanglement.

313
00:37:14,743 --> 00:37:20,343
A. Despite marked knotting of the cords, vigorous twins were delivered by cesarean.

314
00:37:20,343 --> 00:37:25,031
B. Preoperative sonogram of this pregnancy shows entwined cords.

315
00:37:25,031 --> 00:37:29,918
C. This finding is accentuated with application of color Doppler.

316
00:37:29,918 --> 00:37:33,493
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Julie Lo.

317
00:37:33,493 --> 00:37:44,418
In the United States, mothers with monoamnionic twins are often admitted at 24 to 28 weeks’ gestation to begin 1 hour of daily fetal heart rate monitoring .

318
00:37:44,418 --> 00:37:51,181
Optimal surveillance is unclear and may include nonstress testing or biophysical profile assessment .

319
00:37:51,181 --> 00:37:58,343
Betamethasone, discussed later, is considered to promote pulmonary maturation (p. 855).

320
00:37:58,343 --> 00:38:09,306
If fetal testing remains reassuring and no other intervening indications arise, cesarean delivery is performed at 320/7 to 340/7 weeks’ gestation.

321
00:38:09,306 --> 00:38:17,631
To help manage these many care items, the Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine (2020) offers a prenatal checklist.

322
00:38:17,631 --> 00:38:20,568
# Unique and Aberrant Twinning.

323
00:38:20,568 --> 00:38:26,556
Several aberrations in monozygotic twinning result in a spectrum of fetal malformations.

324
00:38:26,556 --> 00:38:32,481
These are traditionally ascribed to incomplete splitting of an embryo into two separate twins.

325
00:38:32,481 --> 00:38:38,818
However, they theoretically may result from early secondary fusion of two separate embryos.

326
00:38:38,818 --> 00:38:43,343
These separated embryos are either symmetrical or asymmetrical.

327
00:38:43,343 --> 00:38:45,231
The spectrum of

328
00:38:45,388 --> 00:38:56,925
asymmetrical twinning includes external parasitic twins, fetus-in-fetu, and twin reversedarterial-perfusion (TRAP) sequence, which is described later (p. 850).

329
00:38:56,875 --> 00:39:05,613
Conjoined Twins Joining of the twins may begin at either pole and produce characteristic forms depending on which body parts are joined or shared .

330
00:39:05,613 --> 00:39:14,350
The frequency of conjoined twins has a prevalence of 1.5 in 100,000 births, and thoracopagus is the most common type .

331
00:39:14,350 --> 00:39:16,775
Figure 48-8.

332
00:39:16,775 --> 00:39:19,225
Types of conjoined twins.

333
00:39:19,225 --> 00:39:24,425
Conjoined twins can be identified using sonography in the first trimester .

334
00:39:24,425 --> 00:39:29,900
This provides an opportunity for parents to decide whether to continue the pregnancy.

335
00:39:29,900 --> 00:39:38,363
During sonographic interrogation, fetal poles are seen to be closely associated and do not change relative position from one another .

336
00:39:38,363 --> 00:39:48,538
Other clues are more than three vessels in the umbilical cord, fewer limbs than expected, spine hyperflexion, bifid fetal pole, and increased nuchal thickness.

337
00:39:48,538 --> 00:39:56,350
Three-dimensional ultrasound, color Doppler, and MR imaging are valuable adjuncts to clarify shared organs .

338
00:39:56,350 --> 00:39:58,975
Figure 48-9.

339
00:39:58,975 --> 00:40:03,875
Sonogram of a conjoined twin pregnancy at 13 weeks’ gestation.

340
00:40:03,875 --> 00:40:09,325
These thoracoomphalopagus twins have two heads but a shared chest and abdomen.

341
00:40:09,325 --> 00:40:14,938
Postnatal surgical separation may be successful if essential organs are not shared.

342
00:40:14,938 --> 00:40:22,663
Conjoined twins may have discordant structural anomalies that further complicate decisions about whether to continue the pregnancy.

343
00:40:22,663 --> 00:40:28,100
Consultation with a pediatric surgeon often assists with parental decisions.

344
00:40:28,100 --> 00:40:32,400
Viable-aged conjoined twins should be delivered by cesarean.

345
00:40:32,400 --> 00:40:39,488
For pregnancy termination, however, vaginal delivery is possible because the union is often pliable.

346
00:40:39,488 --> 00:40:46,025
Still, dystocia is common, and vaginal delivery may be traumatic to the uterus or cervix.

347
00:40:46,025 --> 00:40:56,625
External Parasitic Twins and Fetus-in-fetu Attached to a relatively normal twin, an external parasitic twin is a grossly defective fetus or merely fetal parts.

348
00:40:56,625 --> 00:41:02,675
It usually consists of externally attached supernumerary limbs, often with some viscera.

349
00:41:02,675 --> 00:41:07,100
Classically, however, a functional heart or brain is absent.

350
00:41:07,100 --> 00:41:16,575
Parasitic twins are believed to derive from a dead defective twin, whose surviving tissue attaches to and receives vascular support from the normal co-twin .

351
00:41:16,575 --> 00:41:22,313
In one study, parasitic twins accounted for 4 percent of all conjoined twins .

352
00:41:22,313 --> 00:41:29,225
With fetus-in-fetu, one embryo may enfold early within its co-twin and mainly intraabdominally.

353
00:41:29,225 --> 00:41:34,938
Normal development of this rare parasitic twin usually arrests in the first trimester.

354
00:41:34,938 --> 00:41:39,425
Thus, normal spatial arrangement and many organs are lacking.

355
00:41:39,425 --> 00:41:46,400
Classically, vertebral or axial bones are found in the fetiform mass, whereas a heart and brain are absent.

356
00:41:46,400 --> 00:41:56,038
These masses are thought to be a monozygotic, monochorionic diamnionic twin gestation and are typically supported by large parasitic vessels to the host .

357
00:41:56,038 --> 00:42:00,688
# Monochorionic Twins and Vascular Anastomoses.

358
00:42:00,688 --> 00:42:05,675
All monochorionic placentas likely share some anastomotic connections.

359
00:42:05,675 --> 00:42:12,200
With rare exceptions, anastomoses between twins are unique to monochorionic twin placentas.

360
00:42:12,200 --> 00:42:18,763
However, the number, size, and direction of these seemingly haphazard connections vary markedly .

361
00:42:18,763 --> 00:42:25,975
In one analysis of more than 200 monochorionic placentas, the median number of anastomoses was eight .

362
00:42:25,975 --> 00:42:28,475
Figure 48-10.

363
00:42:28,475 --> 00:42:33,600
Shared placenta from pregnancy complicated by twin-twin transfusion syndrome.

364
00:42:33,600 --> 00:42:37,163
The following color code was applied for injection.

365
00:42:37,163 --> 00:42:45,263
Left twin: yellow = artery, blue = vein; right twin: red = artery, green = vein.

366
00:42:45,263 --> 00:42:54,175
A. Part of the arterial network of the right twin is filled with yellow dye, due to the presence of a small artery-to-artery anastomosis (arrow).

367
00:42:54,175 --> 00:43:00,013
B. Close-up of the lower portion of the placenta displays the yellow dye-filled anastomosis.

368
00:43:00,013 --> 00:43:10,250
Reproduced with permission from De Paepe ME, DeKoninck P, Friedman RM: Vascular distribution patterns in monochorionic twin placentas, Placenta.

369
00:43:10,250 --> 00:43:16,488
2005 Jul;26(6):471–475.

370
00:43:16,488 --> 00:43:25,775
Anastomoses may be artery-to-artery, vein-to-vein, or artery-to-vein communications and are located on the chorionic surface of the placenta.

371
00:43:25,775 --> 00:43:32,988
In contrast to these superficial connections, deep artery-to-vein communications can extend through the capillary

372
00:43:33,148 --> 00:43:35,298
bed of a given villus .

373
00:43:35,248 --> 00:43:46,073
These deep arteriovenous anastomoses create a common villous compartment or “third circulation” that has been identified in approximately half of monochorionic twin placentas.

374
00:43:46,073 --> 00:43:48,810
Figure 48-11.

375
00:43:48,810 --> 00:43:57,123
Anastomoses between twins may be artery-to-vein (AV), artery-to-artery (AA), or vein-to-vein (VV).

376
00:43:57,123 --> 00:44:07,860
Schematic representation of an AV anastomosis in twin-twin transfusion syndrome that forms a “common villous district” or “third circulation” deep within the villous tissue.

377
00:44:07,860 --> 00:44:13,660
Blood from a donor twin may be transferred to a recipient twin through this shared circulation.

378
00:44:13,660 --> 00:44:22,485
This transfer leads to a growth-restricted discordant donor twin (left) with markedly reduced amnionic fluid, causing it to be “stuck.

379
00:44:22,485 --> 00:44:29,623
Whether these anastomoses are dangerous to either twin depends on the degree to which they are hemodynamically balanced.

380
00:44:29,623 --> 00:44:35,560
In those with significant pressure or flow gradients, a shunt will develop between fetuses.

381
00:44:35,560 --> 00:44:51,285
This chronic fetofetal transfusion may result in several clinical syndromes that include twin-twin transfusion syndrome (TTTS), twin anemia–polycythemia sequence (TAPS), and twin reversed-arterial-perfusion (TRAP) sequence.

382
00:44:51,285 --> 00:45:02,735
Twin-Twin Transfusion Syndrome In this syndrome, blood is transfused from a donor twin to its recipient sibling such that the donor may eventually become anemic and its growth may be restricted.

383
00:45:02,735 --> 00:45:11,310
In contrast, the recipient becomes polycythemic and may develop circulatory overload with heart failure manifest as hydrops.

384
00:45:11,310 --> 00:45:18,648
Classically, the donor twin is smaller and pale, and its recipient sibling is larger and has volume excess.

385
00:45:18,648 --> 00:45:25,023
As a result, the recipient neonate may suffer hyperviscosity and occlusive complications.

386
00:45:25,023 --> 00:45:31,873
Polycythemia in the recipient twin may also lead to severe hyperbilirubinemia and kernicterus .

387
00:45:31,873 --> 00:45:37,098
TTTS complicates 10 to 15 percent of monochorionic twins .

388
00:45:37,098 --> 00:45:43,435
Chronic TTTS results from unidirectional flow through deep arteriovenous anastomoses.

389
00:45:43,435 --> 00:45:52,298
Deoxygenated blood from a donor placental artery is pumped into a cotyledon shared by the recipient (see Fig. 48-11).

390
00:45:52,298 --> 00:46:01,385
Once oxygen exchange is completed in the chorionic villus, the oxygenated blood leaves the cotyledon via a placental vein of the recipient twin.

391
00:46:01,385 --> 00:46:12,998
Unless compensated—typically through superficial arterioarterial anastomoses— this unidirectional flow leads to volume depletion in the donor and volume excess in the recipient .

392
00:46:12,998 --> 00:46:21,498
TTTS typically presents in midpregnancy when the donor fetus becomes oliguric from hypovolemia and decreased renal perfusion .

393
00:46:21,498 --> 00:46:31,898
This fetus develops oligohydramnios, and the recipient fetus develops severe hydramnios, presumably due to increased urine production from hypervolemia.

394
00:46:31,898 --> 00:46:44,110
Virtual absence of amnionic fluid in the donor sac prevents fetal motion, giving rise to the descriptive term stuck twin or polyhydramnios–oligohydramnios syndrome—“poly–oli.

395
00:46:44,110 --> 00:46:56,635
This amnionic fluid imbalance is associated with growth restriction, contractures, and pulmonary hypoplasia in the donor twin, and premature rupture of the membranes and heart failure in the recipient.

396
00:46:56,635 --> 00:46:58,748
Fetal Brain Damage.

397
00:46:58,748 --> 00:47:09,760
Cerebral palsy, microcephaly, porencephaly, and multicystic encephalomalacia are serious complications associated with placental vascular anastomoses.

398
00:47:09,760 --> 00:47:19,385
The exact pathogenesis of neurological damage is not fully understood but is likely caused by ischemic necrosis leading to cavitary brain lesions.

399
00:47:19,385 --> 00:47:24,960
In the donor twin, ischemia results from hypotension, anemia, or both.

400
00:47:24,960 --> 00:47:31,960
In the recipient, ischemia develops from blood pressure instability and episodes of profound hypotension .

401
00:47:31,960 --> 00:47:36,560
Cerebral lesions may also be associated with preterm delivery .

402
00:47:36,560 --> 00:47:45,598
In one review of 315 liveborn fetuses from pregnancies with TTTS, cerebral abnormalities were found in 8 percent .

403
00:47:45,598 --> 00:47:56,148
If one twin of an affected pregnancy dies, blood is acutely transfused from high-pressure vessels of the living twin through anastomoses to lowresistance vessels of the dead twin.

404
00:47:56,148 --> 00:48:05,810
This leads rapidly to hypovolemia and possible ischemic antenatal brain damage in the survivor (Fusi, 1990, 1991).

405
00:48:05,810 --> 00:48:11,273
A less likely cause is emboli of thromboplastic material originating from the

406
00:48:11,428 --> 00:48:13,178
dead fetus.

407
00:48:13,128 --> 00:48:21,690
The acuity of hypotension following the death of one twin with TTTS makes successful intervention for the survivor nearly impossible.

408
00:48:21,690 --> 00:48:36,140
Even with delivery immediately after a co-twin demise is recognized, the hypotension that occurs at the moment of death has likely already caused irreversible brain damage (Langer, 1997; Wada, 1998).

409
00:48:36,140 --> 00:48:42,503
As such, immediate delivery is not considered beneficial in the absence of another indication.

410
00:48:42,503 --> 00:48:44,378
Diagnosis.

411
00:48:44,378 --> 00:48:49,065
TTTS is diagnosed based on two sonographic criteria .

412
00:48:49,065 --> 00:48:53,078
First, a monochorionic pregnancy is identified.

413
00:48:53,078 --> 00:49:06,040
Second, hydramnios defined by a largest vertical pocket >8 cm in one sac and oligohydramnios defined by a largest vertical pocket <2 cm in the other twin is found.

414
00:49:06,040 --> 00:49:14,278
Growth discordance or growth restriction may be found with TTTS, but these per se are not considered diagnostic criteria.

415
00:49:14,278 --> 00:49:32,178
Organizations that include the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2021b), Society for Maternal–Fetal Medicine (2013), and North American Fetal Therapy Network recommend sonographic surveillance of pregnancies at risk for TTTS .

416
00:49:32,178 --> 00:49:39,703
These examinations begin at approximately 16 weeks’ gestation, and studies are performed every 2 weeks.

417
00:49:39,703 --> 00:49:51,428
In one study of 108 monochorionic twin pairs, a sonographic evaluation interval >2 weeks was associated with a higher Quintero stage at the time of TTTS diagnosis .

418
00:49:51,428 --> 00:50:00,728
Once identified, TTTS is typically classified by the Quintero (1999) staging system : Figure 48-12.

419
00:50:00,728 --> 00:50:05,540
A. Sonogram of stage I TTTS at 19 weeks’ gestation.

420
00:50:05,540 --> 00:50:14,553
Oligohydramnios in the donor twin sac causes the membrane to essentially wrap around the “stuck twin” and suspend it from the anterior uterine wall.

421
00:50:14,553 --> 00:50:20,078
B. In this same pregnancy, hydramnios is seen in the recipient twin sac.

422
00:50:20,078 --> 00:50:28,203
The measured pocket exceeds 10 cm. C. Stage II TTTS in a donor twin at 17 weeks’ gestation.

423
00:50:28,203 --> 00:50:34,090
Color Doppler highlights the arteries that outline the fetal bladder, which contains no urine.

424
00:50:34,090 --> 00:51:03,565
Stage I—discordant amnionic fluid volumes as described in the earlier paragraph, but urine is still visible sonographically within the bladder of the donor twin • Stage II—criteria of stage I, but urine is not visible within the donor bladder • Stage III—criteria of stage II and abnormal Doppler studies of the umbilical artery, ductus venosus, or umbilical vein • Stage IV—ascites or frank hydrops in either twin • Stage V—demise of either fetus.

425
00:51:03,565 --> 00:51:10,565
In addition to these criteria, cardiac function of the recipient twin also declines with TTTS .

426
00:51:10,565 --> 00:51:20,865
Although fetal echocardiographic findings are not part of the Quintero staging system, many centers routinely perform fetal echocardiography for TTTS.

427
00:51:20,865 --> 00:51:29,165
Theoretically, earlier diagnosis of fetal cardiomyopathy may identify pregnancies that would benefit from early intervention .

428
00:51:29,165 --> 00:51:36,540
One measure, the myocardial performance index (MPI), is a Doppler index of each ventricle’s function.

429
00:51:36,540 --> 00:51:50,228
Scoring systems that include cardiac function have been developed, but their usefulness to predict outcomes remains controversial (Miller, 2021b; Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine, 2013).

430
00:51:50,228 --> 00:51:52,890
Management and Prognosis.

431
00:51:52,890 --> 00:52:01,803
The prognosis for multifetal gestations complicated by TTTS is related to Quintero stage and gestational age at presentation.

432
00:52:01,803 --> 00:52:06,603
Some stage I cases remain stable or regress without intervention.

433
00:52:06,603 --> 00:52:13,490
However, 60 percent progress (Emery, 2016; Stirnemann, 2021).

434
00:52:13,490 --> 00:52:29,378
Conversely, outcomes in those identified at stage III or higher are much worse, and the perinatal loss rate is 70 to 100 percent without intervention (Miller, 2021b; Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine, 2013).

435
00:52:29,378 --> 00:52:39,228
Several therapies available for TTTS include laser ablation of vascular placental anastomoses, amnioreduction, and selective feticide.

436
00:52:39,228 --> 00:52:45,640
Similar to amniocentesis , amnioreduction is needle drainage of excess amnionic fluid.

437
00:52:45,640 --> 00:52:49,815
These techniques have been evaluated in randomized trials.

438
00:52:49,815 --> 00:52:58,078
The Eurofetus trial included 142 women with severe TTTS diagnosed before 26 weeks’ gestation.

439
00:52:58,078 --> 00:53:05,365
Participants were randomly assigned to laser ablation of vascular anastomoses or to serial amnioreduction .

440
00:53:05,365 --> 00:53:09,315
Laser ablation yielded a 76-percent rate of

441
00:53:09,484 --> 00:53:16,471
survival to age 6 months for at least one twin compared with a 51-percent rate with amnioreduction.

442
00:53:16,421 --> 00:53:31,496
Analyses of most randomized studies confirm better neonatal outcomes with laser therapy compared with selective amnioreduction (Marwan, 2019; Roberts, 2008; Rossi, 2008, 2009).

443
00:53:31,496 --> 00:53:44,546
However, evaluation of twins from the Eurofetus trial through 6 years of age did not demonstrate an additional survival benefit beyond 6 months or improved neurological outcomes in those treated with laser .

444
00:53:44,546 --> 00:53:51,834
At this time, laser ablation of anastomoses is preferred for severe TTTS (stages II–IV).

445
00:53:51,834 --> 00:54:03,146
Optimal therapy for stage I disease is controversial, and laser ablation or expectant surveillance is an option (Emery, 2015; Stirnemann, 2021).

446
00:54:03,146 --> 00:54:10,846
Of techniques, an ablation method may laser individual anastomoses or may ablate the entire the vascular equator.

447
00:54:10,846 --> 00:54:16,146
This equator is the border between twin vasculature on the chorionic surface.

448
00:54:16,146 --> 00:54:23,709
Equatorial ablation, which is referred to as a Solomon technique, reduces the likelihood of TTTS recurrence.

449
00:54:23,709 --> 00:54:30,346
This method also lowers rates of TAPS, which can be a lasertherapy sequelae and described next .

450
00:54:30,346 --> 00:54:35,046
After laser therapy, close ongoing surveillance is necessary.

451
00:54:35,046 --> 00:54:42,784
Weekly ultrasound and Doppler studies are recommended (Marwan, 2019; Miller 2021b).

452
00:54:42,784 --> 00:54:50,034
These evaluations monitor fetal growth, amnionic fluid volumes, placental function, and anemia.

453
00:54:50,034 --> 00:55:01,471
Delivery timing is usually influenced by TTTS recurrence, fetal-growth restriction, or by abnormal Doppler velocimetry values, which reflect poor placental function .

454
00:55:01,471 --> 00:55:11,396
Another treatment option, selective fetal reduction, has generally been considered if severe amnionic fluid and growth disturbances develop before 20 weeks.

455
00:55:11,396 --> 00:55:16,396
In such cases, both fetuses typically will die without intervention.

456
00:55:16,396 --> 00:55:22,471
Any substance injected into one twin may affect the other twin because of shared circulations.

457
00:55:22,471 --> 00:55:30,321
Thus, for the fetus chosen for reduction, feticidal methods aim to occlude the umbilical vein or umbilical cord.

458
00:55:30,321 --> 00:55:45,609
Radiofrequency ablation, fetoscopic ligation, or coagulation with laser, monopolar, or bipolar energy are options (Challis, 1999; Chang, 2009; Parra-Cordero, 2016).

459
00:55:45,609 --> 00:55:51,709
Even after these procedures, however, risks to the remaining fetus are still appreciable .

460
00:55:51,709 --> 00:55:56,234
Early termination of the entire pregnancy is yet another option.

461
00:55:56,234 --> 00:56:08,134
Twin Anemia–Polycythemia Sequence Chronic fetofetal transfusion underlies this form, which is characterized by significant hemoglobin differences between donor and recipient twins.

462
00:56:08,134 --> 00:56:17,421
Similar to TTTS, the donor twin in spontaneous TAPS is anemic and usually smaller than the recipient twin, which is polycythemic.

463
00:56:17,421 --> 00:56:23,984
However, TAPS lacks the discrepancies in amnionic fluid volumes typical of TTTS.

464
00:56:23,984 --> 00:56:31,696
Spontaneous TAPS can develop at any gestational age and complicates 1 to 6 percent of monochorionic twins .

465
00:56:31,696 --> 00:56:45,521
Iatrogenic TAPS develops in up to 13 percent of pregnancies after laser ablation of the placenta and usually develops within 5 weeks of a procedure (Lewi, 2013; Tollenaar, 2021).

466
00:56:45,521 --> 00:56:54,671
In iatrogenic TAPS, the former TTTS recipient twin usually becomes anemic, whereas the former donor becomes polycythemic .

467
00:56:54,671 --> 00:57:06,471
Sonographically measuring blood flow velocity in the fetal middle cerebral artery (MCA) can accurately identify fetal anemia (Chap. 14, 262).

468
00:57:06,471 --> 00:57:15,009
Antenatally, TAPS is diagnosed by discordant MCA peak systolic velocity (PSV) values between twins.

469
00:57:15,009 --> 00:57:26,084
Specifically, an MCA-PSV value that is >1.5 multiples of the median (MoM) in the donor twin and <1.0 MoM in the recipient twin .

470
00:57:26,084 --> 00:57:33,546
Others have suggested alternative threshold values (Khalil, 2020; Tollenaar, 2019).

471
00:57:33,546 --> 00:57:45,021
A staging system for TAPS has been proposed, and higher stage is associated with increased perinatal mortality rate (Slaghekke, 2010; Tollenaar, 2021).

472
00:57:45,021 --> 00:57:58,071
Screening for spontaneous TAPS is controversial, as improvements in perinatal outcomes have not been demonstrated (Khalil, 2016; Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine, 2013).

473
00:57:58,071 --> 00:58:08,121
Management options include expectant care, delivery, laser surgery, intrauterine transfusion, selective feticide, and pregnancy termination.

474
00:58:08,121 --> 00:58:13,659
A clinical trial evaluating fetoscopic laser therapy for TAPS is currently

475
00:58:13,828 --> 00:58:15,428
ongoing.

476
00:58:15,378 --> 00:58:20,053
Antenatal surveillance mirrors that just described for TTTS.

477
00:58:20,053 --> 00:58:28,115
Again, delivery timing is usually influenced by worsening fetal growth or by abnormal Doppler velocimetry values.

478
00:58:28,115 --> 00:58:36,078
Postnatal treatment often requires blood transfusion for the donor twin and partial exchange transfusion of the recipient twin.

479
00:58:36,078 --> 00:58:49,840
Twin Reversed-arterial-perfusion Sequence Also known as acardiac twinning, this rare, serious complication of monochorionic multifetal gestation has an estimated incidence of 1 case in 35,000 births.

480
00:58:49,840 --> 00:59:02,065
With classic TRAP sequence, the twin pair is a normally formed donor twin that shows features of heart failure and a grossly malformed recipient twin that lacks a heart (acardius) and other structures.

481
00:59:02,065 --> 00:59:10,840
In one theory, the TRAP sequence is caused by a large artery-to-artery placental shunt, often also accompanied by a vein-to-vein shunt .

482
00:59:10,840 --> 00:59:18,190
Within the single, shared placenta, arterial perfusion pressure of the donor twin exceeds that in the recipient twin.

483
00:59:18,190 --> 00:59:24,965
The recipient thus receives reversed blood flow containing deoxygenated arterial blood from its co-twin .

484
00:59:24,965 --> 00:59:33,115
This “used” arterial blood reaches the recipient twin through its umbilical arteries and preferentially goes to its iliac vessels.

485
00:59:33,115 --> 00:59:40,003
Thus, only the lower body is perfused, and disrupted growth and development of the upper body follows.

486
00:59:40,003 --> 00:59:56,815
In these cases, failed head growth is called acardius acephalus; a partially developed head with identifiable limbs is called acardius myelacephalus; and failure of any recognizable structure to form is acardius amorphous, which is shown in Figure 48-14.

487
00:59:56,815 --> 01:00:07,765
Because of this vascular connection, the normal donor twin must not only support its own circulation but also must pump blood to and through the underdeveloped acardiac recipient.

488
01:00:07,765 --> 01:00:16,803
This may lead to cardiomegaly and high-output heart failure in the donor twin (Fox, 2007; Marwan, 2019).

489
01:00:16,803 --> 01:00:19,653
Figure 48-13.

490
01:00:19,653 --> 01:00:23,040
Twin reversed-arterial-perfusion sequence.

491
01:00:23,040 --> 01:00:31,615
In the TRAP sequence, there is usually a normally formed donor twin that has features of heart failure, and a recipient twin that lacks a heart.

492
01:00:31,615 --> 01:00:40,390
It is hypothesized that the TRAP sequence follows a large artery-to-artery placental shunt, often also accompanied by a vein-to-vein shunt.

493
01:00:40,390 --> 01:00:50,978
Within the single, shared placenta, perfusion pressure of the donor twin overpowers that in the recipient twin, who thus receives reverse blood flow from its twin sibling.

494
01:00:50,978 --> 01:00:59,503
The “used” arterial blood that reaches the recipient twin preferentially goes to its iliac vessels and thus perfuses only the lower body.

495
01:00:59,503 --> 01:01:03,340
This disrupts growth and development of the upper body.

496
01:01:03,340 --> 01:01:06,128
Figure 48-14.

497
01:01:06,128 --> 01:01:11,115
Photograph of an acardiac twin weighing 475 grams.

498
01:01:11,115 --> 01:01:17,290
The underdeveloped head is indicated by the black arrow, and its details are shown in the inset.

499
01:01:17,290 --> 01:01:20,728
A yellow clamp is seen on its umbilical cord.

500
01:01:20,728 --> 01:01:28,515
Its viable donor co-twin was delivered vaginally at 36 weeks and weighed 2325 grams.

501
01:01:28,515 --> 01:01:33,040
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Michael D. Hnat.

502
01:01:33,040 --> 01:01:37,828
In the past, the donor-twin mortality rate exceeded 50 percent.

503
01:01:37,828 --> 01:01:45,265
This stemmed largely from complications of prematurity or from a prolonged high-output state leading to cardiac failure .

504
01:01:45,265 --> 01:01:49,515
Risk is directly related to size of the acardiac twin.

505
01:01:49,515 --> 01:01:54,053
When the acardiac twin is large, treatment is generally offered.

506
01:01:54,053 --> 01:02:03,828
Radiofrequency ablation (RFA) is the preferred modality of therapy, and contemporary reports now suggest improved perinatal outcomes.

507
01:02:03,828 --> 01:02:14,953
The North American Fetal Therapy Network reviewed their experiences with 98 cases from 1998 to 2008 in which RFA of the umbilical cord was performed.

508
01:02:14,953 --> 01:02:22,753
Median gestational age at delivery was 37 weeks’ gestation, and 80 percent of donor neonates survived .

509
01:02:22,753 --> 01:02:27,103
# Hydatidiform Mole with Coexisting Normal Fetus.

510
01:02:27,103 --> 01:02:33,615
This rare gestation contains one normal fetus, and its co-twin is a complete molar pregnancy.

511
01:02:33,615 --> 01:02:45,240
It must be differentiated from a partial molar pregnancy, which is a singleton, triploid fetus and its placenta composed of molar tissue (Fig. 13-4, p. 238).

512
01:02:45,240 --> 01:02:49,028
Diagnosis in the first half of pregnancy is common.

513
01:02:49,028 --> 01:02:57,065
Sonographically, a normal-appearing twin is accompanied by its co-twin, which is a large placenta containing multiple small

514
01:02:57,220 --> 01:02:59,407
anechoic cysts.

515
01:02:59,357 --> 01:03:05,795
Often, these pregnancies are terminated, but pregnancy continuation is increasingly adopted.

516
01:03:05,795 --> 01:03:09,507
The live birth rate from one review was 50 percent .

517
01:03:09,507 --> 01:03:24,182
The risk of gestational trophoblastic neoplasia (GTN), which is a malignant sequelae of hydatidiform mole, is similar whether the pregnancy is terminated or not (Massardier, 2009; Sebire, 2002).

518
01:03:24,182 --> 01:03:29,932
Given the limited number of cases, robust data for firm recommendations are lacking.

519
01:03:29,932 --> 01:03:43,145
Discussed in Chapter 13 (p. 237), complications of expectant management include vaginal bleeding, hyperemesis gravidarum, thyrotoxicosis, and early-onset preeclampsia.

520
01:03:43,145 --> 01:03:49,582
Many of these complications result in preterm birth with its attendant adverse perinatal outcomes.

521
01:03:49,582 --> 01:03:56,095
Logically, close antepartum and postpartum surveillance is needed for those continuing the pregnancy.

522
01:03:56,095 --> 01:04:02,670
Postpartum GTN surveillance is essential and described in Chapter 13 (p. 240).

523
01:04:02,670 --> 01:04:04,695
Discordant Growth.

524
01:04:04,695 --> 01:04:10,645
Fetal size inequality develops in approximately 15 percent of twin gestations .

525
01:04:10,645 --> 01:04:17,757
Generally, as the weight difference within a twin pair grows, the perinatal mortality rate rises proportionately.

526
01:04:17,757 --> 01:04:23,957
Earlier discordancy and monochorionicity pose increased mortality risks for the smaller twin.

527
01:04:23,957 --> 01:04:38,932
Specifically, with discordant growth identified at or before 20 weeks’ gestation in studies, 8 to 15 percent of the growth-restricted fetuses die (Couck, 2020; Curado, 2020; D’Antonio, 2018).

528
01:04:38,932 --> 01:04:41,482
# Pathogenesis.

529
01:04:41,482 --> 01:04:48,270
The etiology of growth discordance in monochorionic twins likely differs from that in dichorionic twins.

530
01:04:48,270 --> 01:04:58,445
First, in monochorionic twins, the single placenta is not always equally shared, and this leads to higher discordant growth rates than in dichorionic pairs .

531
01:04:58,445 --> 01:05:08,832
In cases with TTTS, discordancy in monochorionic twins is usually attributed to placental anastomoses that cause a perfusion imbalance between twins.

532
01:05:08,832 --> 01:05:13,932
Reduced perfusion of the donor twin can diminish placental and fetal growth .

533
01:05:13,932 --> 01:05:21,482
Last, monochorionic twins at times can be discordant in size because they are discordant for structural anomalies.

534
01:05:21,482 --> 01:05:24,157
Figure 48-15.

535
01:05:24,157 --> 01:05:27,845
Marked growth discordance in monochorionic twins.

536
01:05:27,845 --> 01:05:31,445
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Laura Greer.

537
01:05:31,445 --> 01:05:36,695
In dichorionic twins, discordancy may result from various factors.

538
01:05:36,695 --> 01:05:43,395
Dizygotic fetuses may have different genetic growth potential, especially if they are of opposite genders.

539
01:05:43,395 --> 01:05:52,270
Second, because the placentas are separate and require more implantation space, one placenta might have a suboptimal implantation site.

540
01:05:52,270 --> 01:06:00,845
Additionally, umbilical cord abnormalities such as velamentous insertion, marginal insertion, or vasa previa may play a role .

541
01:06:00,845 --> 01:06:07,495
One study showed that the incidence of severe discordancy is twice as great in triplets as it is in twins .

542
01:06:07,495 --> 01:06:12,845
This supports the view that in-utero crowding promotes multifetal growth restriction.

543
01:06:12,845 --> 01:06:16,145
Placental pathology may be contributory.

544
01:06:16,145 --> 01:06:29,745
In one study of 668 twin placentas, a strong relationship between histological placental abnormalities and birthweight discordancy was observed in dichorionic but not monochorionic twin pregnancies .

545
01:06:29,745 --> 01:06:32,145
# Diagnosis.

546
01:06:32,145 --> 01:06:37,732
Antenatal size discordancy between twins can be best determined sonographically.

547
01:06:37,732 --> 01:06:43,095
Crown-rump length differences are not reliable predictors for birthweight discordance .

548
01:06:43,095 --> 01:06:48,232
Thus, most discordancy surveillance begins after the first trimester.

549
01:06:48,232 --> 01:06:54,795
One common method uses sonographic fetal biometry to compute an estimated weight for each twin .

550
01:06:54,795 --> 01:07:03,457
Percent discordancy is then calculated as the weight of the larger twin minus the weight of the smaller twin, divided by the weight of the larger twin.

551
01:07:03,457 --> 01:07:13,882
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2021b) defines discordance as an estimated fetal weight difference >20 percent.

552
01:07:13,882 --> 01:07:16,107
# Management.

553
01:07:16,107 --> 01:07:22,107
The risk for adverse perinatal outcomes in the setting of growth discordance remains controversial.

554
01:07:22,107 --> 01:07:39,320
Some suggest that twin gestations with discordant growth between two fetuses that are appropriately grown for gestational age are not at increased risk for adverse outcomes (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2021b; Appleton, 2007).

555
01:07:39,320 --> 01:07:44,432
Others have shown an increased risk for adverse fetal outcomes but not for poor

556
01:07:44,596 --> 01:07:50,858
neonatal outcomes (Amaru, 2004; D’Antonio, 2018).

557
01:07:50,808 --> 01:08:04,033
However, accumulated data suggest that weight discordancy exceeding 25 to 30 percent most accurately predicts an adverse perinatal outcome (Cohen, 2001; Chen, 2019).

558
01:08:04,033 --> 01:08:12,733
At Parkland Hospital, twin weight-discordancy values from 1370 delivered twin pairs were stratified by percentage increments .

559
01:08:12,733 --> 01:08:26,146
The incidence of respiratory distress syndrome, intraventricular hemorrhage, seizures, periventricular leukomalacia, sepsis, and necrotizing enterocolitis rose directly with the percentage of weight discordancy.

560
01:08:26,146 --> 01:08:31,921
Rates of these conditions grew substantially if discordancy exceeded 25 percent.

561
01:08:31,921 --> 01:08:42,971
The relative risk of fetal death increased significantly to 5.6 if weight discordancy was >30 percent and rose to 18.9 if >40 percent.

562
01:08:42,971 --> 01:08:50,446
Nonstress testing and biophysical profile assessment have all been recommended in management of twin growth discordancy.

563
01:08:50,446 --> 01:09:02,083
If significant discordancy is identified in a monochorionic twin pair, umbilical artery Doppler studies in the smaller fetus may help guide management (Gratacós, 2007).

564
01:09:02,083 --> 01:09:08,583
Data are limited to establish the optimal timing of delivery of twins with size discordancy alone.

565
01:09:08,583 --> 01:09:12,908
At advanced gestational ages, delivery can be pursued.

566
01:09:12,908 --> 01:09:24,858
Selective Fetal-growth Restriction Restricted growth of one twin fetus is termed selective fetal-growth restriction (sFGR) and usually develops late in the second and early third trimester.

567
01:09:24,858 --> 01:09:35,508
Some diagnose sFGR if the abdominal circumference (AC) measurement difference exceeds 20 mm or if fetal-growth discordance is >20 percent .

568
01:09:35,508 --> 01:09:46,546
If sFGR is diagnosed, weekly testing of fetal wellbeing, evaluation of amnionic fluid volume, and umbilical artery Doppler velocimetry are undertaken.

569
01:09:46,546 --> 01:09:56,596
Investigators have correlated Doppler results with placental findings and with the degree of sFGR to predict fetal outcome (Gratacós, 2012).

570
01:09:56,596 --> 01:10:00,921
These correlations have yielded categories of sFGR.

571
01:10:00,921 --> 01:10:09,158
Type I shows positive end-diastolic flow, a smaller degree of weight discordance, and a relatively benign clinical course.

572
01:10:09,158 --> 01:10:17,721
Type II displays persistently absent end-diastolic flow in the smaller twin and carries a high risk of deterioration and demise.

573
01:10:17,721 --> 01:10:22,683
Type III has intermittently absent or reversed end-diastolic flow.

574
01:10:22,683 --> 01:10:33,621
Because of large artery-to-artery anastomoses associated with the placentas in this last category, type III is associated with a lower risk of deterioration than type II.

575
01:10:33,621 --> 01:10:39,208
In all evaluated cases, unequally shared placenta was noted to some degree.

576
01:10:39,208 --> 01:10:46,646
Serial evaluation of fetal growth is performed every 3 weeks (Chap 47, p. 829).

577
01:10:46,646 --> 01:10:59,483
At Parkland Hospital, daily inpatient surveillance is undertaken in women with fetal-growth restriction in one twin or with twin discordancy exceeding 25 percent in the setting of a twin with restricted growth.

578
01:10:59,483 --> 01:11:01,421
Fetal Demise.

579
01:11:01,421 --> 01:11:09,008
At any time during multifetal pregnancy, one or more fetuses may die, either simultaneously or sequentially.

580
01:11:09,008 --> 01:11:14,096
Most causes stem from complications of fetal anomaly or chorionicity.

581
01:11:14,096 --> 01:11:29,508
Related to the latter, compared with dichorionic twins, monochorionic pairs suffer higher rates of sFGR, TTTS, or TAPS from unequal vascular anastomoses, and monoamnionic pairs can die from cord entanglement.

582
01:11:29,508 --> 01:11:35,196
When death is early, it may manifest as a vanishing twin (p. 843).

583
01:11:35,196 --> 01:11:45,133
For the survivor, the risk of death after the first trimester is not increased, and the pregnancy requires no additional surveillance for this specific indication.

584
01:11:45,133 --> 01:11:56,533
In a slightly more advanced gestation, the dead fetus may be compressed appreciably—fetus compressus, or it may be flattened remarkably through desiccation—fetus papyraceus .

585
01:11:56,533 --> 01:11:59,346
Figure 48-16.

586
01:11:59,346 --> 01:12:05,021
This fetus papyraceus is a tan ovoid mass compressed against the fetal membranes.

587
01:12:05,021 --> 01:12:08,633
Anatomical parts can be identified as marked.

588
01:12:08,633 --> 01:12:15,546
Demise of this twin had been noted during sonographic examination performed at 17 weeks’ gestation.

589
01:12:15,546 --> 01:12:19,046
Its viable co-twin delivered at 40 weeks.

590
01:12:19,046 --> 01:12:23,346
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Michael V. Zaretsky.

591
01:12:23,346 --> 01:12:34,696
In gestations past 20 weeks, the stillbirth rate in twins in the United States was 1.4 percent of live births and exceeded the 0.6-percent rate in singletons .

592
01:12:34,696 --> 01:12:36,146
After

593
01:12:36,316 --> 01:12:51,991
a single intrauterine fetal demise (sIUFD) in these later gestations, risks to the remaining co-twin include death, preterm birth, and neurological injury (D’Antonio, 2017; Mackie, 2019).

594
01:12:51,941 --> 01:13:01,703
With the last, acute hypovolemia from volume shifts within placental anastomoses occurs immediately, and neurological injury is not preventable.

595
01:13:01,703 --> 01:13:15,328
Related to co-twin death, monochorionic diamnionic twins with an sIUFD were 16 times more likely to experience death of the co-twin than were dichorionic twins with an sIUFD in one large series .

596
01:13:15,328 --> 01:13:35,028
In most studies, the rate of co-twin demise after sIUFD declines after 26 to 28 weeks’ gestation and with advancing gestational age (Mackie, 2019; McPherson, 2012; Southwest Thames Obstetric Research Collaborative, 2012; Wood, 2014).

597
01:13:35,028 --> 01:13:42,341
The rate of preterm birth following sIUFD is increased but is similar between mono- and dichorionic twins.

598
01:13:42,341 --> 01:13:50,253
Advancing rates are seen at later gestational ages (D’Antonio, 2017; Ong, 2006).

599
01:13:50,253 --> 01:13:56,091
The neurological prognosis for a surviving co-twin is influenced by chorionicity.

600
01:13:56,091 --> 01:14:10,728
In one review of diamnionic twin pregnancies complicated by sIUFD before 34 weeks, neurodevelopmental morbidity was 29 percent in monochorionic twins and was threefold higher than in dichorionic gestations .

601
01:14:10,728 --> 01:14:21,841
In other series with sIUFD after 34 weeks, the likelihood of neurological deficits was essentially the same between monochorionic and dichorionic twin pregnancies .

602
01:14:21,841 --> 01:14:33,816
Because of this significant neurological risk, if one fetus of a monochorionic twin gestation dies after the first trimester but before viability, pregnancy termination can be considered .

603
01:14:33,816 --> 01:14:40,853
After viability, ultimate delivery timing balances the risk of prematurity against cotwin demise.

604
01:14:40,853 --> 01:14:46,616
Early management emphasizes maternal and co-twin safety and gestation prolongation .

605
01:14:46,616 --> 01:14:51,653
Antenatal corticosteroids for survivor lung maturity can be considered.

606
01:14:51,653 --> 01:14:57,528
For sIUFD occurring after 34 weeks’ gestation, delivery is reasonable .

607
01:14:57,528 --> 01:15:03,503
Last, the death of one fetus could theoretically trigger coagulation defects in the mother.

608
01:15:03,503 --> 01:15:10,578
Only a few cases of maternal coagulopathy after a single fetal death in a twin pregnancy have been reported.

609
01:15:10,578 --> 01:15:16,591
This is probably because the surviving twin is usually delivered within a few weeks of the demise .

610
01:15:16,591 --> 01:15:18,478
Prenatal Care.

611
01:15:18,478 --> 01:15:26,703
At Parkland Hospital clinics, women with multifetal gestations are seen every 2 weeks beginning at 22 weeks’ gestation.

612
01:15:26,703 --> 01:15:36,553
One imperative is preterm delivery prevention, and a digital cervical examination is performed at each visit to screen for cervical shortening or dilation.

613
01:15:36,553 --> 01:15:45,253
The maternal diet should provide additional requirements for calories, protein, minerals, vitamins, and essential fatty acids.

614
01:15:45,253 --> 01:15:55,953
The Institute of Medicine (IOM) (2009) recommends a 37- to 54-lb weight gain for women with twins and a normal body mass index.

615
01:15:55,953 --> 01:16:12,553
The daily recommended augmented caloric intake for women with twins is 40 to 45 kcal/kg/d. Diets contain 20 percent protein, 40 percent carbohydrate, and 40 percent fat divided into three meals and three snacks daily.

616
01:16:12,553 --> 01:16:24,141
Based on upper-intake levels from the Food and Nutrition Board of the IOM, one review endorsed supplementation of calcium, magnesium, zinc, and vitamins C, D, and E .

617
01:16:24,141 --> 01:16:36,041
Discussed in Chapter 40 (p. 705), low-dose aspirin prophylaxis is recommended in women at high risk of preeclampsia, which includes those with a multifetal pregnancy .

618
01:16:36,041 --> 01:16:44,266
An 81-mg oral daily dose is initiated between 12 and 28 weeks’ gestation and is continued until delivery .

619
01:16:44,266 --> 01:16:51,616
Prenatal screening for fetal aneuploidy is carried out as described in Chapter 17 (p. 334).

620
01:16:51,616 --> 01:16:59,703
Thus, the combined test or secondary screening is done and interpreted with value thresholds set for multifetal pregnancies.

621
01:16:59,703 --> 01:17:12,941
Noninvasive prenatal screening with cfDNA also is acceptable (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2020d; Judah, 2021; Khalil, 2021).

622
01:17:12,941 --> 01:17:19,678
Interpretation caveats for those with a vanishing twin were described earlier (p. 844).

623
01:17:19,678 --> 01:17:26,216
To screen for structural anomalies, a midpregnancy anatomic sonography survey is performed.

624
01:17:26,216 --> 01:17:33,203
In addition, monochorionic twins undergo echocardiography because of an increased risk for cardiac

625
01:17:33,364 --> 01:17:35,101
anomalies .

626
01:17:35,051 --> 01:17:40,764
As noted earlier (p. 844), fetal growth is assessed with sonography.

627
01:17:40,764 --> 01:17:46,614
Amnionic fluid volume is quantified using the deepest vertical pocket in each sac.

628
01:17:46,614 --> 01:17:59,939
A measurement <2 cm is considered oligohydramnios, and a measurement >8 cm is considered hydramnios (Duryea, 2017; Hernandez, 2012).

629
01:17:59,939 --> 01:18:05,639
Twin pregnancies are at increased risk for stillbirth compared to singleton gestations.

630
01:18:05,639 --> 01:18:17,326
The intrauterine fetal death rate is two- to threefold higher in monochorionic pregnancies compared with dichorionic gestations (Lee, 2008; Morikawa, 2012).

631
01:18:17,326 --> 01:18:28,364
Cheong-See and colleagues (2016) reported a higher stillbirth rate in monochorionic gestations compared with dichorionic gestations beyond 34 weeks’ gestation.

632
01:18:28,364 --> 01:18:40,401
Outpatient weekly nonstress test or biophysical profile may help lower the rate of intrauterine death in twin gestations (Booker, 2015; Burgess, 2014).

633
01:18:40,401 --> 01:18:52,976
Weekly antenatal surveillance is undertaken at 36 weeks’ gestation for uncomplicated dichorionic pregnancies and at 32 weeks’ gestation or uncomplicated monochorionic gestations .

634
01:18:52,976 --> 01:18:58,039
However, all antenatal testing schemes have high false-positive rates.

635
01:18:58,039 --> 01:19:06,539
In cases of abnormal testing in one twin and normal results in another, iatrogenic preterm delivery remains a major concern.

636
01:19:06,539 --> 01:19:13,001
Therefore, patients are carefully counseled prior to initiating antenatal testing in twins.

637
01:19:13,001 --> 01:19:14,901
Preterm Birth.

638
01:19:14,901 --> 01:19:20,501
Gestational length shortens and preterm birth risk rises with accruing fetal number.

639
01:19:20,501 --> 01:19:29,201
In 2019 in the United States, the preterm birth rate in twins and triplets was 60 and 98 percent, respectively .

640
01:19:29,201 --> 01:19:42,476
One review showed that approximately 60 percent of preterm births in twins are indicated, a third result from spontaneous labor, and 10 percent follow preterm prelabor rupture of membranes (PPROM) .

641
01:19:42,476 --> 01:19:50,514
Of risks, preterm birth rates vary with chorionicity and are higher with monochorionic compared with dichorionic twins.

642
01:19:50,514 --> 01:20:01,201
Other implicated factors are prior preterm birth, adolescence, nulliparity, obesity, and diabetes (Marleen, 2018, 2021).

643
01:20:01,201 --> 01:20:03,376
# Prediction.

644
01:20:03,376 --> 01:20:10,014
One goal of multifetal prenatal care is to identify women likely to experience preterm delivery.

645
01:20:10,014 --> 01:20:20,476
To and associates (2006) sonographically measured cervical length in 1163 twin pregnancies at 22 to 24 weeks’ gestation.

646
01:20:20,476 --> 01:20:34,239
Rates of preterm delivery before 32 weeks were 66 percent in those with cervical lengths of 10 mm; 24 percent for lengths of 20 mm; and only 1 percent for lengths of 40 mm.

647
01:20:34,239 --> 01:20:42,689
In one review, a cervical length <20 mm was most accurate for predicting birth before 34 weeks’ gestation.

648
01:20:42,689 --> 01:20:49,251
The specificity was 97 percent, and positive likelihood ratio was 9.0 .

649
01:20:49,251 --> 01:21:02,314
Notably, a closed internal os by digital examination predicted postponed delivery equally well as the combination of a normal sonographically measured cervical length and negative fetal fibronectin test result .

650
01:21:02,314 --> 01:21:08,826
Unfortunately, cervical length assessment in twin pregnancies has not led to improved outcomes .

651
01:21:08,826 --> 01:21:18,276
The Society for Maternal–Fetal Medicine (2016) recommends against routine cervical length screening in multifetal gestations.

652
01:21:18,276 --> 01:21:22,701
Fetal fibronectin levels also may predict preterm birth .

653
01:21:22,701 --> 01:21:29,214
Again, neonatal outcomes are not improved in twins, and this screening tool is not recommended .

654
01:21:29,214 --> 01:21:31,364
# Prevention.

655
01:21:31,364 --> 01:21:39,514
Bed Rest In general, most strategies for preterm birth prevention are ineffective for singleton and multifetal pregnancies .

656
01:21:39,514 --> 01:21:45,939
Of options, bed rest with or without hospitalization does not prolong multifetal pregnancy.

657
01:21:45,939 --> 01:21:53,976
In one metaanalysis, the practice did not reduce the risk of preterm birth (da Silva Lopes, 2017).

658
01:21:53,976 --> 01:22:01,514
At Parkland Hospital, elective hospitalization was compared with outpatient management, and no advantages were found .

659
01:22:01,514 --> 01:22:11,989
Importantly, however, almost half of women managed as outpatients required admission for specific indications such as hypertension or threatened preterm delivery.

660
01:22:11,989 --> 01:22:28,326
Limited physical activity, early work leave, more frequent health-care visits, serial sonographic examinations, and structured maternal education regarding preterm delivery risks have been advocated to reduce preterm birth rates in women with multiple fetuses.

661
01:22:28,326 --> 01:22:31,901
However, little evidence suggests that these

662
01:22:32,068 --> 01:22:35,468
practices substantially change this outcome.

663
01:22:35,418 --> 01:22:41,343
Prophylactic Tocolysis This has not been studied extensively in multifetal pregnancies.

664
01:22:41,343 --> 01:22:54,680
In one review of prophylactic oral beta-mimetic therapy that included 374 twin pregnancies, treatment did not reduce the rate of twins delivering before 37 or before 34 weeks’ gestation .

665
01:22:54,680 --> 01:23:07,005
In light of the Food and Drug Administration warning against the use of oral terbutaline because of maternal side effects, the prophylactic use of beta-mimetic drugs in multifetal gestations seems unwarranted.

666
01:23:07,005 --> 01:23:22,730
Progesterone Therapy 17-alpha-hydroxyprogesterone caproate (17-OHPC) injections are not effective for multifetal gestations (Caritis, 2009; Combs, 2011; Rouse, 2007).

667
01:23:22,730 --> 01:23:38,680
Moreover, women carrying twins and having a cervical length <36 mm or a length <25 mm did not benefit despite their greater risk for preterm birth (Durnwald, 2010; Senat, 2013).

668
01:23:38,680 --> 01:23:53,980
Vaginal micronized progesterone in various formulations in randomized studies has also proved ineffective for preterm birth prevention before 34 weeks in a general twin population (Norman, 2009; Rode, 2011).

669
01:23:53,980 --> 01:24:07,130
For women with a short cervix, subgroup analysis and a metaanalysis show conflicting results regarding benefits (D’Antonio, 2021; Klein, 2011; Rehal, 2021).

670
01:24:07,130 --> 01:24:14,955
At Parkland Hospital, current management of multifetal gestations does not typically include any progesterone therapy.

671
01:24:14,955 --> 01:24:21,880
Cervical Cerclage Prophylactic cerclage does not improve perinatal outcome in women with twin pregnancies.

672
01:24:21,880 --> 01:24:31,505
Studies have included women with and without a short cervix (D’Antonio, 2021; Jarde, 2017; Newman, 2002).

673
01:24:31,505 --> 01:24:39,868
Physical examination–indicated cerclage in women with a second-trimester twin gestation and a dilated cervix may be beneficial.

674
01:24:39,868 --> 01:24:57,418
Roman and coworkers (2016, 2020) reported that women undergoing rescue cerclage plus prophylactic antibiotic and indomethacin administration before 24 weeks’ gestation had lower preterm birth and perinatal mortality rates than those without a cerclage.

675
01:24:57,418 --> 01:25:10,068
Pessary The silicone Arabin vaginal pessary encircles and theoretically compresses the cervix, alters the inclination of the cervical canal, and relieves direct pressure on the internal cervical os.

676
01:25:10,068 --> 01:25:26,205
In a study of its use in women with a short cervix between 18 and 22 weeks’ gestation, a subgroup analysis of 23 women with twins showed a significant reduction in the delivery rate before 32 weeks compared with the rate in 23 control pregnancies .

677
01:25:26,205 --> 01:25:34,718
In another randomized trial of twins, women treated with a cervical pessary had significantly fewer births before 34 weeks .

678
01:25:34,718 --> 01:25:40,430
Other randomized studies comparing pessary against expectant care are less favorable.

679
01:25:40,430 --> 01:25:49,093
In one trial with 1180 uncomplicated twin pregnancies, pessary use failed to alter the preterm birth rate .

680
01:25:49,093 --> 01:25:54,380
Another trial of 813 unselected women had similar findings.

681
01:25:54,380 --> 01:26:05,368
However, delivery rates before 32 weeks were lower—29 versus 14 percent—in a subset of women with a cervical length <38 mm .

682
01:26:05,368 --> 01:26:14,218
Instead, other trials show no benefit from pessary use in this subgroup (Nicolaides, 2016; Norman, 2021).

683
01:26:14,218 --> 01:26:22,805
At this time, pessary use is not recommended by the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2021b).

684
01:26:22,805 --> 01:26:24,880
# Treatment.

685
01:26:24,880 --> 01:26:38,668
Tocolytic therapy to help halt preterm labor in multifetal pregnancy does not measurably improve neonatal outcomes (Canadian Preterm Labor Investigators Group, 1992; Yamasmit, 2015).

686
01:26:38,668 --> 01:26:46,830
Moreover, tocolytic therapy in women with a multifetal pregnancy entails higher maternal risk than in singleton pregnancy.

687
01:26:46,830 --> 01:26:57,443
This stems in part from augmented pregnancy-induced hypervolemia, which raises cardiac demands and increases the susceptibility to iatrogenic pulmonary edema .

688
01:26:57,443 --> 01:27:10,318
In one study, all women with a multifetal gestation treated with a beta-mimetic drug for preterm labor had more cardiovascular complications—43 versus 4 percent—than women with singletons .

689
01:27:10,318 --> 01:27:24,480
In a retrospective analysis, nifedipine tocolysis in 58 singleton and 32 twin pregnancies led to higher incidences of side effects such as maternal tachycardia in women with twins—19 versus 9 percent .

690
01:27:24,480 --> 01:27:29,368
Antenatal corticosteroids for fetal lung maturation have not been

691
01:27:29,524 --> 01:27:32,724
well studied in multifetal gestations.

692
01:27:32,674 --> 01:27:40,299
Of studies, many compare outcomes among singletons and twins whose mothers either did or did not receive this therapy.

693
01:27:40,299 --> 01:27:49,511
In one large retrospective study evaluating betamethasone, neonatal morbidity rates were reduced in both twin and singleton treated groups .

694
01:27:49,511 --> 01:28:02,111
Another similar comparison found reduced rates of periventricular hemorrhage rates in treated groups but not improved respiratory distress syndrome (RDS) rates (Gonçalves-Ferri, 2021).

695
01:28:02,111 --> 01:28:13,561
Similarly, in another study, short-term neurological outcome was better in treated twin and singletons, but rates of mortality, RDS, and cerebral palsy were not reduced .

696
01:28:13,561 --> 01:28:22,949
However, in one study solely with twins, the rate of RDS or of composite neonatal morbidity was not lower with corticosteroid therapy .

697
01:28:22,949 --> 01:28:28,936
These authors posited pharmacokinetics in twin gestation as a potential explanation.

698
01:28:28,936 --> 01:28:35,099
Currently, recommended use of agents is the same for multifetal and singleton gestations .

699
01:28:35,099 --> 01:28:40,361
This therapy is described fully in Chapter 45 (p. 802).

700
01:28:40,361 --> 01:28:43,886
# Preterm Prelabor Membrane Rupture.

701
01:28:43,886 --> 01:28:48,449
The frequency of PPROM rises with increasing plurality.

702
01:28:48,449 --> 01:28:59,561
In a populationbased study of more than 290,000 live births, the proportion of preterm birth complicated by PPROM was 13 percent in singletons.

703
01:28:59,561 --> 01:29:09,361
This rate was 17, 20, 20, and 100 percent in twins, triplets, quadruplets, and higher-order multiples, respectively .

704
01:29:09,361 --> 01:29:20,861
For both twin and singleton pregnancies, most studies show comparable neonatal risk with expectant management, which is outlined in Chapter 45 (p. 798) .

705
01:29:20,861 --> 01:29:26,586
However, the time between births, often termed latency, is shorter with twins.

706
01:29:26,586 --> 01:29:37,561
With PPROM after 24 weeks’ gestation, the median number of days to subsequent delivery was 4 days for twins compared with 7 days for singletons in one study .

707
01:29:37,561 --> 01:29:43,161
After 30 weeks’ gestation, this latency for twins is significantly shortened .

708
01:29:43,161 --> 01:29:46,374
# Delayed Delivery of Second Twin.

709
01:29:46,374 --> 01:29:54,124
Rarely, after preterm birth of one fetus, it may be advantageous for undelivered fetus(es) to remain in utero.

710
01:29:54,124 --> 01:29:58,599
This may be especially so at periviable gestational ages.

711
01:29:58,599 --> 01:30:03,824
However, this advantage must be balanced against substantial maternal risk.

712
01:30:03,824 --> 01:30:10,449
In one systematic review, nearly 40 percent of mothers suffered complications with delayed delivery .

713
01:30:10,449 --> 01:30:15,524
These include infection, postpartum hemorrhage, and placental abruption.

714
01:30:15,524 --> 01:30:26,136
From population-based data of delayed deliveries, the median latency duration was 6 days (range 6 to 107 days) in 200 twin pregnancies .

715
01:30:26,136 --> 01:30:32,486
In all studies, retained fetuses had better overall survival rates than the first-born neonate.

716
01:30:32,486 --> 01:30:37,561
In one series of 38 twins, the mean latency was 19 days.

717
01:30:37,561 --> 01:30:45,411
Of first-born twins born at <25 weeks’ gestation, none survived, but 50 percent of their co-twins did.

718
01:30:45,411 --> 01:30:53,136
Beyond 25 weeks, survival rates were 65 for first-born and 95 percent for second-born twins .

719
01:30:53,136 --> 01:31:00,986
In their series of 28 twin pairs, cerclage, antibiotic therapy, and tocolysis did not improve outcomes .

720
01:31:00,986 --> 01:31:07,886
If delayed delivery is attempted, counseling should include the potential for serious maternal complications.

721
01:31:07,886 --> 01:31:19,324
The range of gestational age for which benefits outweigh the risks for delayed delivery is likely narrow, and gestations of 22 to 24 weeks would seem the most probable to benefit .

722
01:31:19,324 --> 01:31:24,024
In our experience, good candidates for delayed delivery are rare.

723
01:31:24,024 --> 01:31:26,136
Labor And Delivery.

724
01:31:26,136 --> 01:31:28,624
# Delivery Timing.

725
01:31:28,624 --> 01:31:37,986
Several factors affect this timing and include gestational age, fetal growth, maternal complications, lung maturity, and stillbirth risk.

726
01:31:37,986 --> 01:31:46,024
The substantially greater stillbirth rate in monochorionic monoamnionic twins was discussed earlier (p. 845).

727
01:31:46,024 --> 01:31:59,436
In dichorionic diamnionic twins, stillbirth rates rose to 10.6 deaths per 1000 pregnancies at 386/7 weeks’ gestation in a systematic review of more than 30,000 twin pairs.

728
01:31:59,436 --> 01:32:08,811
The peak rate in monochorionic diamnionic twins was 9.6 deaths per 1000 pregnancies at 376/7 weeks .

729
01:32:08,811 --> 01:32:15,086
In uncomplicated twins, stillbirth risk is balanced against the morbidity of prematurity.

730
01:32:15,086 --> 01:32:21,749
Pulmonary maturation is usually synchronous in twins and occurs several weeks earlier than in singletons .

731
01:32:21,749 --> 01:32:26,249
However, in some cases, pulmonary function may differ.

732
01:32:26,249 --> 01:32:28,624
With sFGR, the

733
01:32:28,780 --> 01:32:35,455
smaller, stressed twin has lower rates of RDS but higher rates of bronchopulmonary dysplasia .

734
01:32:35,405 --> 01:32:50,205
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2021b) recommends delivery at 380/7 to 386/7 weeks’ gestation for uncomplicated dichorionic diamnionic twin pregnancies.

735
01:32:50,205 --> 01:32:59,280
Uncomplicated monochorionic diamnionic twin pregnancies can be delivered between 340/7 and 376/7 weeks.

736
01:32:59,280 --> 01:33:08,455
For monochorionic monoamnionic twin pregnancies, delivery is recommended at 320/7 to 340/7 weeks.

737
01:33:08,455 --> 01:33:21,192
At Parkland Hospital, we generally follow these recommendations and do not routinely deliver monochorionic diamnionic twin pregnancies before 37 weeks unless another obstetrical indication develops.

738
01:33:21,192 --> 01:33:23,642
# Preparations.

739
01:33:23,642 --> 01:33:29,555
A litany of complications may be encountered during labor and delivery of multiple fetuses.

740
01:33:29,555 --> 01:33:43,967
Rates of uterine contractile dysfunction, abnormal fetal presentation, umbilical cord prolapse, placenta previa, placental abruption, emergent operative delivery, and postpartum hemorrhage rates from uterine atony are higher.

741
01:33:43,967 --> 01:33:56,930
Moreover, second twins at term have worse composite neonatal outcomes compared with outcomes of their co-twin regardless of delivery method (Smith, 2007; Thorngren-Jerneck, 2001).

742
01:33:56,930 --> 01:34:03,305
All of these must be anticipated, and thus certain precautions and special arrangements are prudent.

743
01:34:03,305 --> 01:34:10,680
These include the following: 1. An appropriately trained obstetrical attendant should remain with the mother throughout labor.

744
01:34:10,680 --> 01:34:14,305
Continuous electronic monitoring is preferable.

745
01:34:14,305 --> 01:34:20,442
If membranes are ruptured and the cervix dilated, the presenting fetus is monitored internally.

746
01:34:20,442 --> 01:34:26,592
2. An intravenous infusion system capable of delivering fluid rapidly is established.

747
01:34:26,592 --> 01:34:36,655
In the absence of hemorrhage, lactated Ringer or an aqueous dextrose solution is infused at a rate of 60 to 125 mL/hr. 3.

748
01:34:36,655 --> 01:34:40,480
Blood for transfusion is readily available if needed.

749
01:34:40,480 --> 01:34:49,492
4. An obstetrician skilled in intrauterine identification of fetal parts and in intrauterine manipulation of a fetus should be present.

750
01:34:49,492 --> 01:35:00,530
5. A sonography machine is readily available to evaluate the presentation and position of the fetuses during labor and to image the remaining fetus after delivery of the first.

751
01:35:00,530 --> 01:35:11,705
6. An anesthesia team is immediately available in the event that emergent cesarean delivery is necessary or that intrauterine manipulation is required for vaginal delivery.

752
01:35:11,705 --> 01:35:22,217
7. For each fetus, at least one attendant who is skilled in resuscitation and care of newborns and who has been appropriately informed of the case should be immediately available.

753
01:35:22,217 --> 01:35:31,467
8. The delivery area should provide adequate space for the nursing, obstetrical, anesthesia, and pediatric team members to work effectively.

754
01:35:31,467 --> 01:35:39,580
Equipment must be on site to provide emergent anesthesia, operative intervention, and maternal and neonatal resuscitation.

755
01:35:39,580 --> 01:35:42,817
# Analgesia and Anesthesia.

756
01:35:42,817 --> 01:35:50,367
For multifetal pregnancies, decisions regarding analgesia and anesthesia must factor planned route of delivery.

757
01:35:50,367 --> 01:36:01,530
Other potential problems may stem from preterm labor, preeclampsia, desultory labor, need for intrauterine manipulation, and postpartum uterine atony and hemorrhage.

758
01:36:01,530 --> 01:36:12,917
Labor epidural analgesia is ideal because it provides excellent pain relief and can be rapidly extended cephalad if internal podalic version or cesarean delivery is required.

759
01:36:12,917 --> 01:36:24,242
If general anesthesia becomes necessary for intrauterine manipulation during vaginal birth, uterine relaxation can be accomplished rapidly with a halogenated inhalation agent .

760
01:36:24,242 --> 01:36:32,167
Some clinicians use intravenous or sublingual nitroglycerin or intravenous terbutaline to achieve uterine relaxation.

761
01:36:32,167 --> 01:36:37,417
If used, these agents are usually best administered by the anesthesia team.

762
01:36:37,417 --> 01:36:40,117
# Fetal Presentation.

763
01:36:40,117 --> 01:36:45,767
For labor and delivery, the fetal presentations are best determined sonographically.

764
01:36:45,767 --> 01:36:54,392
If active labor is confirmed, the decision for vaginal or cesarean delivery is reached, and fetal presentation is a major factor.

765
01:36:54,392 --> 01:37:04,192
Among the possible presentation combinations, those most common at admission for delivery are cephalic-cephalic, cephalic-breech, and cephalic-transverse.

766
01:37:04,192 --> 01:37:12,280
At Parkland Hospital between 2008 and 2013, 71 percent of twin pregnancies had a cephalic

767
01:37:12,436 --> 01:37:17,286
presentation of the first fetus at the time of labor and delivery admission.

768
01:37:17,236 --> 01:37:22,561
During parturition, fetal presentation of a second twin can be unstable.

769
01:37:22,561 --> 01:37:36,248
For them, compound or footling breech presentations and face or brow attitudes are relatively common and even more so if fetuses are small or numerous, amnionic fluid is excessive, or maternal parity is high.

770
01:37:36,248 --> 01:37:40,311
Cord prolapse also is frequent in these circumstances.

771
01:37:40,311 --> 01:37:42,773
# Delivery Route.

772
01:37:42,773 --> 01:37:58,923
Cephalic First Twin With cephalic–cephalic presentation, general consensus supports consideration of vaginal birth in a laboring woman near term (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2021b; D’Alton, 2010).

773
01:37:58,923 --> 01:38:10,273
From studies, planned cesarean delivery does not improve neonatal outcome when both twins are cephalic (de Castro, 2016; Schmitz, 2017).

774
01:38:10,273 --> 01:38:15,373
With trials of labor, vaginal delivery rates approximate 80 percent.

775
01:38:15,373 --> 01:38:21,711
With cephalic–noncephalic presentation, the optimal delivery route remains controversial.

776
01:38:21,711 --> 01:38:30,848
Patient selection and provider expertise with vaginal breech delivery is crucial and described in Chapter 28 (p. 527).

777
01:38:30,848 --> 01:38:36,136
As a result, one common option is cesarean delivery of both twins.

778
01:38:36,136 --> 01:38:45,348
Less often, after spontaneous vaginal delivery of a first twin, intrapartum external cephalic version of the second twin can be performed.

779
01:38:45,348 --> 01:38:58,973
In case series, this practice, compared with internal podalic version, was associated with higher rates of intrapartum cesarean delivery and fetal distress (Gocke, 1989; Smith 1997).

780
01:38:58,973 --> 01:39:06,723
With internal podalic version, a hand placed into the uterus grasps fetal feet to deliver the fetus by breech extraction .

781
01:39:06,723 --> 01:39:15,011
Last and least desirable is vaginal delivery of the first but cesarean delivery of the second twin due to intrapartum complications.

782
01:39:15,011 --> 01:39:22,636
These are umbilical cord prolapse, placental abruption, contracting cervix, or nonreassuring fetal heart rate.

783
01:39:22,636 --> 01:39:32,186
Most but not all studies report the worst composite fetal outcomes for this scenario (Alexander, 2008; Rossi, 2011).

784
01:39:32,186 --> 01:39:35,011
Figure 48-17.

785
01:39:35,011 --> 01:39:37,523
Internal podalic version.

786
01:39:37,523 --> 01:39:44,961
Upward pressure on the head by an abdominal hand is applied (not shown) as downward traction is exerted on the feet.

787
01:39:44,961 --> 01:39:57,586
For cephalic–noncephalic twins, each with birthweights >1500 g and gestational ages >32 weeks, several reports attest to the safety of vaginal delivery.

788
01:39:57,586 --> 01:40:09,348
In one study of 5915 pregnancies with a cephalic first twin and either cephalic or noncephalic second twin, 25 percent planned for cesarean delivery .

789
01:40:09,348 --> 01:40:16,011
The other 75 percent planned a trial of vaginal delivery, which was successful in 80 percent.

790
01:40:16,011 --> 01:40:28,911
Interestingly, perinatal mortality and morbidity rates were significantly higher in the planned cesarean delivery group delivered at <37 weeks—5.2 versus 3.0 percent, respectively.

791
01:40:28,911 --> 01:40:37,186
Additionally, for those delivered vaginally, neonatal outcomes were similar for second twins that were cephalic or noncephalic .

792
01:40:37,186 --> 01:40:45,361
The Twin Birth Study had similar inclusion criteria and randomly assigned women to planned vaginal or planned cesarean delivery.

793
01:40:45,361 --> 01:40:48,923
Perinatal outcomes were similar in both groups.

794
01:40:48,923 --> 01:40:56,361
For those delivered vaginally, neonatal outcomes were similar for second twins that were cephalic or noncephalic .

795
01:40:56,361 --> 01:41:12,436
For neonates weighing <1500 g compared with those weighing more, comparable or even better fetal outcomes with vaginal delivery compared with cesarean delivery have been reported (Mol, 2020; Sentilhes, 2015).

796
01:41:12,436 --> 01:41:17,411
However, rates of urgent operations for the second twin may be higher .

797
01:41:17,411 --> 01:41:28,173
Other investigators advocate cesarean delivery for both fetuses of a cephalic–noncephalic twin pair (Armson, 2006; Hoffmann, 2012).

798
01:41:28,173 --> 01:41:36,898
Yang and coworkers (2005a,b) studied 15,185 cephalic–noncephalic pairs.

799
01:41:36,898 --> 01:41:47,823
The risks of asphyxia-related neonatal deaths and morbidity were higher in the group in which both twins were delivered vaginally compared with the group in which both twins were delivered surgically.

800
01:41:47,823 --> 01:41:55,473
Breech First Twin Problems with the first twin presenting as a breech are similar to those encountered with a singleton breech fetus.

801
01:41:55,473 --> 01:42:04,123
First, the fetal body can be small, and delivery of the extremities and trunk through an inadequately effaced and dilated cervix can leave the

802
01:42:04,276 --> 01:42:07,901
relatively larger head trapped above the cervix.

803
01:42:07,851 --> 01:42:12,876
This is more likely when disproportion between the head and body is significant.

804
01:42:12,876 --> 01:42:19,238
Examples are preterm or growth-restricted fetuses or those with macrocephaly from hydrocephaly.

805
01:42:19,238 --> 01:42:23,663
Second, umbilical cord prolapse is an ever-present risk.

806
01:42:23,663 --> 01:42:30,988
Last, twin fetuses may become locked together during delivery if the first presents breech and the second cephalic.

807
01:42:30,988 --> 01:42:39,113
As the breech of the first twin descends through the birth canal, the chin locks between the neck and chin of the second cephalic-presenting co-twin.

808
01:42:39,113 --> 01:42:48,313
This phenomenon is rare, and Cohen and coworkers (1965) described it only once in 817 twin gestations.

809
01:42:48,313 --> 01:42:56,051
If these problems are anticipated or identified, cesarean delivery is often preferred with a viable-sized fetus.

810
01:42:56,051 --> 01:43:04,738
Even without these problems, many obstetricians perform cesarean delivery if the first twin presents as breech, and this is our practice.

811
01:43:04,738 --> 01:43:13,613
However, data support the safety of vaginal delivery for twins older than 32 weeks and weighing >1500 g .

812
01:43:13,613 --> 01:43:19,388
In one study, cesarean delivery was planned in 1169 such pairs.

813
01:43:19,388 --> 01:43:25,801
Vaginal delivery was planned in 298 pairs and was successful in 64 percent.

814
01:43:25,801 --> 01:43:30,113
Cesarean delivery of the second twin was done in 1 percent.

815
01:43:30,113 --> 01:43:35,613
Neonatal mortality or morbidity measures did not differ between delivery groups .

816
01:43:35,613 --> 01:43:39,113
# Labor Augmentation or Induction.

817
01:43:39,113 --> 01:43:47,226
In general, active labor with twins progresses more slowly in both nulliparas and multiparas compared with that in singletons .

818
01:43:47,226 --> 01:43:51,088
Secondstage labor of the first twin also is longer .

819
01:43:51,088 --> 01:43:58,201
In women with twins who meet all criteria for oxytocin administration, labor augmentation is suitable .

820
01:43:58,201 --> 01:44:10,976
For labor induction, studies have found that oxytocin alone or in combination with cervical ripening can safely be used in twin gestations (Hamou, 2016; Ko, 2014).

821
01:44:10,976 --> 01:44:25,451
Compared with prelabor cesarean delivery, others have found higher maternal morbidity rates with labor induction, and ultimate cesarean delivery rates approximate 40 percent (Dougan, 2020; Grossman, 2021).

822
01:44:25,451 --> 01:44:36,813
In an analysis of twin births in the United States, induction rates of twin pregnancies have declined from nearly 14 percent in 1999 to 10 percent in 2008 .

823
01:44:36,813 --> 01:44:43,851
Generally, at Parkland Hospital we do not augment or induce labor in women with a multifetal gestation.

824
01:44:43,851 --> 01:44:50,301
Concerns include risks for uterine rupture from an overdistended uterus and postpartum hemorrhage.

825
01:44:50,301 --> 01:44:57,188
In suitable candidates with a strong desire for vaginal birth, amniotomy induction has been one option.

826
01:44:57,188 --> 01:44:59,776
# Vaginal Delivery.

827
01:44:59,776 --> 01:45:07,026
After delivery of the firstborn, one clamp is placed near the neonate, and another is placed nearer the placenta.

828
01:45:07,026 --> 01:45:19,226
Until the last fetus is delivered, each cord must remain clamped to prevent fetal hypovolemia and anemia caused by blood leaving the placenta via anastomoses and then through an unclamped cord.

829
01:45:19,226 --> 01:45:24,326
Cord blood is generally not collected until after delivery of all fetuses.

830
01:45:24,326 --> 01:45:31,976
After the second neonate is delivered, two plastic clamps are placed on the placenta’s cord to differentiate it from the first.

831
01:45:31,976 --> 01:45:39,513
In higher-order deliveries, color-tagged or alphabetically labeled clamps can be simpler than adding additional clamps.

832
01:45:39,513 --> 01:45:43,051
This same practice holds for cesarean delivery.

833
01:45:43,051 --> 01:45:51,351
At this time, evidence is insufficient to recommend for or against delayed umbilical cord clamping in multifetal gestations .

834
01:45:51,351 --> 01:46:06,188
Following vaginal delivery of the first twin, the presenting part of the second twin, its size, and its relationship to the birth canal should be quickly ascertained by combined abdominal, vaginal, and, at times, intrauterine examination.

835
01:46:06,188 --> 01:46:08,901
Sonography is a valuable aid.

836
01:46:08,901 --> 01:46:16,188
If the fetal head or the breech is fixed in the birth canal, moderate fundal pressure is applied and membranes are ruptured.

837
01:46:16,188 --> 01:46:22,888
Immediately afterward, digital examination of the cervix is repeated to exclude cord prolapse.

838
01:46:22,888 --> 01:46:25,388
Labor is allowed to resume.

839
01:46:25,388 --> 01:46:33,451
If contractions do not begin within approximately 10 minutes, dilute oxytocin may be used to stimulate contractions.

840
01:46:33,451 --> 01:46:40,376
The preferred interval between delivery of the first and second twins is frequently cited as <30 minutes.

841
01:46:40,376 --> 01:46:45,513
In some studies, longer intertwin intervals are associated with poorer

842
01:46:45,676 --> 01:46:51,513
outcome of the second twin (Leung, 2002; Stein, 2008).

843
01:46:51,463 --> 01:46:59,388
Others have correlated fetal heart rate tracing abnormalities during the intertwin interval rather than its length with poorer outcome .

844
01:46:59,388 --> 01:47:14,688
If the occiput or breech presents immediately over the pelvic inlet, but is not fixed in the birth canal, the presenting part can often be guided into the pelvis by one hand in the vagina, while a second hand on the uterine fundus exerts moderate pressure caudally.

845
01:47:14,688 --> 01:47:19,701
A presenting shoulder may be gently converted into a cephalic presentation.

846
01:47:19,701 --> 01:47:26,351
Alternatively, with abdominal manipulation, an assistant can guide the presenting part into the pelvis.

847
01:47:26,351 --> 01:47:30,613
Sonography can aid guidance and allow heart rate monitoring.

848
01:47:30,613 --> 01:47:42,063
If the occiput or breech is not over the pelvic inlet and cannot be so positioned by gentle pressure or if appreciable uterine bleeding develops, delivery of the second twin can be problematic.

849
01:47:42,063 --> 01:47:57,101
To obtain a favorable outcome, an obstetrician skilled in intrauterine fetal manipulation and anesthesia personnel skilled in providing anesthesia to effectively relax the uterus for vaginal delivery of a noncephalic second twin are essential .

850
01:47:57,101 --> 01:48:05,326
To take maximum advantage of the dilated cervix before the uterus contracts and the cervix retracts, delay should be avoided.

851
01:48:05,326 --> 01:48:18,088
Prompt cesarean delivery of the second fetus is preferred if no one present is skilled in the performance of internal podalic version or if anesthesia that will provide effective uterine relaxation is not immediately available.

852
01:48:18,088 --> 01:48:21,901
# Trial of Labor after Cesarean Delivery.

853
01:48:21,901 --> 01:48:29,651
The main concern with trial of labor after cesarean delivery (TOLAC) is uterine rupture from a distended uterus.

854
01:48:29,651 --> 01:48:37,951
One metaanalysis found rates of rupture and of successful vaginal birth were comparable with those for TOLAC in singleton gestations .

855
01:48:37,951 --> 01:48:46,988
Other studies also support the safety of TOLAC for selected women with twins (Cahill, 2005; Ford, 2006).

856
01:48:46,988 --> 01:48:54,151
In one assessment of 186 women undergoing TOLAC, two thirds delivered both twins vaginally.

857
01:48:54,151 --> 01:49:00,026
Of failed attempts, 45 percent underwent cesarean for delivery of the second twin .

858
01:49:00,026 --> 01:49:17,326
According to the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019), evidence currently does not suggest an increased risk of uterine rupture, and women with twins and one previous cesarean delivery with a low transverse incision may be considered TOLAC candidates.

859
01:49:17,326 --> 01:49:21,963
At Parkland Hospital, we recommend repeat cesarean delivery.

860
01:49:21,963 --> 01:49:25,201
# Cesarean Delivery Technique.

861
01:49:25,201 --> 01:49:32,401
Several unusual intraoperative problems can arise during cesarean delivery of twins or higher-order multiples.

862
01:49:32,401 --> 01:49:40,501
Supine hypotension is common, and thus gravidas are positioned in a left lateral tilt to deflect uterine weight off the aorta.

863
01:49:40,501 --> 01:49:48,463
A low transverse hysterotomy is preferable if the incision can be made large enough to allow atraumatic delivery of all fetuses.

864
01:49:48,463 --> 01:49:52,963
Piper forceps can be used if a second twin is presenting breech.

865
01:49:52,963 --> 01:50:00,726
In some cases, a vertical hysterotomy beginning as low as possible in the lower uterine segment may be advantageous.

866
01:50:00,726 --> 01:50:16,226
For example, if a fetus is transverse with its back down and the arms are inadvertently delivered first, it is much easier and safer to extend a vertical uterine incision upward than to extend a transverse incision laterally or to make a “T” incision vertically.

867
01:50:16,226 --> 01:50:19,626
# Triplet or Higher-order Gestation.

868
01:50:19,626 --> 01:50:24,726
Fetal heart rate monitoring during labor with triplet pregnancies is challenging.

869
01:50:24,726 --> 01:50:33,576
A scalp electrode can be attached to the presenting fetus, but it is difficult to ensure that the other two fetuses are each being monitored separately.

870
01:50:33,576 --> 01:50:39,513
With vaginal delivery, the first neonate is usually born with little or no manipulation.

871
01:50:39,513 --> 01:50:44,626
Subsequent fetuses, however, are delivered according to the presenting part.

872
01:50:44,626 --> 01:50:54,201
This often requires complicated obstetrical maneuvers such as total breech extraction with or without internal podalic version or even cesarean delivery.

873
01:50:54,201 --> 01:50:59,901
Associated with malposition of fetuses is an increased incidence of cord prolapse.

874
01:50:59,901 --> 01:51:06,963
Moreover, reduced placental perfusion and hemorrhage from separating placentas are more likely during delivery.

875
01:51:06,963 --> 01:51:11,476
Data from the Consortium on Safe Labor found that only 17

876
01:51:11,644 --> 01:51:17,731
percent of women with triplet gestation attempting vaginal birth were actually delivered vaginally.

877
01:51:17,681 --> 01:51:22,544
In these births, composite neonatal morbidity rates were increased .

878
01:51:22,544 --> 01:51:30,219
In 7000 triplet pregnancies, vaginal delivery also was associated with a higher perinatal mortality rate .

879
01:51:30,219 --> 01:51:37,469
For all these reasons, we deliver most pregnancies complicated by three or more fetuses by cesarean delivery.

880
01:51:37,469 --> 01:51:50,019
Vaginal delivery is reserved for those circumstances in which survival is not expected because fetuses are markedly immature or abnormal or maternal complications make cesarean delivery hazardous to the mother.

881
01:51:50,019 --> 01:51:59,056
Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (2009) national data show that 94 percent of triplets are delivered by cesarean.

882
01:51:59,056 --> 01:52:06,831
Other cases series describe more positive outcomes (Grobman, 1998; Peress, 2019).

883
01:52:06,831 --> 01:52:23,106
In sum, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2021b) notes vaginal birth may be considered for uncomplicated triplet pregnancies, if the first fetus is cephalic and attendants are experienced with multifetal vaginal birth.

884
01:52:23,106 --> 01:52:30,344
As with twins, candidate fetuses should weigh >1500 g. Reducing Fetal Number.

885
01:52:30,344 --> 01:52:40,194
For multifetal gestations, multifetal pregnancy reduction (MPR) aims to lower fetal number to improve survival rates of the remaining fetuses.

886
01:52:40,194 --> 01:52:48,269
Instead, with selective reduction, early pregnancy intervention focuses on a fetus with an anomaly or serious health risk.

887
01:52:48,269 --> 01:52:57,569
With selective termination, the indications are analogous to those for selective reduction, but interventions are performed at a later gestational age .

888
01:52:57,569 --> 01:53:02,769
Selective reduction or MPR is typically done in the late first trimester.

889
01:53:02,769 --> 01:53:21,694
This gestational age is chosen because most spontaneous abortions have already occurred, the remaining fetuses are large enough to be evaluated sonographically, the amount of devitalized fetal tissue remaining after the procedure is small, and the risk of aborting the entire pregnancy as a result of the procedure is low.

890
01:53:21,694 --> 01:53:27,194
Skill in sonographically guiding needles through the mother’s abdomen and uterus is required.

891
01:53:27,194 --> 01:53:35,294
For reductions of fetuses with their own chorion, potassium chloride is used and is injected into the fetal heart or thorax.

892
01:53:35,294 --> 01:53:40,306
Entering or traversing the sacs of fetuses picked for retention is avoided.

893
01:53:40,306 --> 01:53:53,706
For monochorionic fetuses and potentially shared vasculature, cord-occlusion is used, and with radiofrequency ablation (RFA), the needle is inserted into the intrafetal portion of the umbilical cord.

894
01:53:53,706 --> 01:54:04,919
Before reduction procedures, discussion should include the morbidity and mortality rates associated with expectant care, entire pregnancy termination, or selective fetal reduction .

895
01:54:04,919 --> 01:54:24,644
With these options, specific risks include (1) loss of all remaining fetuses, (2) abortion or retention of the wrong fetus, (3) damage without death to a fetus, (4) preterm labor, (5) fetuses with discordant or growth restriction, and (6) maternal complications.

896
01:54:24,644 --> 01:54:35,944
With reduction procedures, uncommon potential complications are infection, hemorrhage, or disseminated intravascular coagulopathy because of retained products of conception.

897
01:54:35,944 --> 01:54:39,294
# Multifetal Pregnancy Reduction.

898
01:54:39,294 --> 01:54:45,594
In most cases of MPR, higher-order gestations are reduced by one or more fetuses.

899
01:54:45,594 --> 01:54:54,319
With triplets, reduction to twins or a singleton lowers the rate of preterm birth before 34 weeks compared with expectant management.

900
01:54:54,319 --> 01:55:01,494
Miscarriage rates are not higher (Anthoulakis, 2017; Morlando, 2015).

901
01:55:01,494 --> 01:55:12,106
With even higher-order multiples, spontaneous loss and preterm birth rates also decline after MPR (Evans, 2014; Liu, 2020).

902
01:55:12,106 --> 01:55:16,631
Twin gestations also may be reduced to a singleton pregnancy.

903
01:55:16,631 --> 01:55:28,444
Maternal comorbidities or concerns for monochorionic twin complications, described earlier, are frequent indications (Rao, 2021; Vieira, 2019).

904
01:55:28,444 --> 01:55:32,444
# Selective Reduction or Selective Termination.

905
01:55:32,444 --> 01:55:40,419
If multiple fetuses are discordant for anatomical or genetic anomalies, elimination of the abnormal fetus is an option.

906
01:55:40,419 --> 01:55:46,094
Other indications are severe TTTS, TAPS, TRAP, or sFGR.

907
01:55:46,094 --> 01:55:56,094
Because abnormalities are often not fully delineated until the second trimester, selective termination is performed later in gestation than selective reduction

908
01:55:56,260 --> 01:55:58,710
and entails greater risk.

909
01:55:58,660 --> 01:56:05,047
This procedure is therefore usually not performed unless the abnormality is severe but not lethal.

910
01:56:05,047 --> 01:56:11,622
In some cases, termination is considered because the abnormal fetus may jeopardize the normal one.

